(1278, 517, 'Happiness Hurts Me', 'Think of Depeche Mode''s "Golden Period" with a bit more depression and brilliant buzzy electronica melodies. What more can you say about an album titled "Happiness Hurts Me" ?\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Self Delusion/Happiness Hurts Me/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Self Delusion/Happiness Hurts Me/cover_200.jpg', 1125903600, 4),
(1279, 518, 'Mozart in the Age of Enlightenment', 'The New York Times describes Seth Carlin''s interpretations of Mozart as "sparklingly transparent," and this album definitely showcases his gift for celebrating the bright, buoyant side of Mozart''s work. Carlin''s own sense of joy in the music is palpable throughout the performance, and he has an absolutely impeccable sense of timing.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Seth Carlin/Mozart in the Age of Enlightenment/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Seth Carlin/Mozart in the Age of Enlightenment/cover_200.jpg', 1166601600, 30),
(1280, 518, 'Schubert - Music for fortepiano four hands', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Seth Carlin/Schubert - Music for fortepiano four hands/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Seth Carlin/Schubert - Music for fortepiano four hands/cover_200.jpg', 1108108800, 16),
(1281, 518, 'Schubert - Works for Solo Fortepiano', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Seth Carlin/Schubert - Works for Solo Fortepiano/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Seth Carlin/Schubert - Works for Solo Fortepiano/cover_200.jpg', 1108108800, 25),
(1282, 519, 'Chroma', 'Heavily inspired by artists like BT, The Prodigy, and Hybrid, Chroma ebbs and flows through periods of melodic ambiance and intense, aggressive beats. Seemingly disparate textures and tones are combined adeptly to create an impressively diverse, yet cohesive and always intelligent electronica album. Go on a melodic breakbeat electronica journey with SGX in his 2006 release Chroma.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/Chroma/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/Chroma/cover_200.jpg', 1321084800, 14),
(1283, 519, 'Hero of the Grey Area', 'SGX intersperses electro, breakbeat, and industrial beats between lush orchestrations, growling synth basses, and transporting soundscapes. "Hero" is at the same time electronic and synthetic plus natural and human. This is the most focused, cohesive album by SGX focusing on instrumental pieces.\n\nUnlike his previous albums such as Chroma and Synesthetic which feature a mixture of not only genres, but tones, Hero of the Grey Area exudes a more homogeneous tone and feeling while still exploring fusing ideas from many genres. This is music for listening and sometimes takes its time exploring an idea in the vein of BT''s "This Binary Universe."\n\nHero of the Grey Area features "A Meal for a Whale" - a track made with only a software sequencer/sampler, a piano, and the audio toy/game Electroplankton for the Nintendo DS as sound sources. SGX effects, samples, records, and manipulates his way through this at once lo-fi and lush recording creating tides of relaxed beauty and exuberance.\n\nAlso featured is the four-movement "i.transmit.this" - an exercise in re-use and modulation of interchangeable musical ideas. SGX begins with simple overlapping piano melodies that appear and re-appear in later parts of the piece as different instruments and ideas, inspiring new ideas. "I Transmit This" branches, grows new limbs, and finally returns to the main concepts which in the end bear the influences of the new ideas that were themselves inspired by the original concepts.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/Hero of the Grey Area/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/Hero of the Grey Area/cover_200.jpg', 1315378800, 7),
(1284, 519, 'SGXRMX3', 'Five remixers plus SGX have reinterpreted songs from SGX''s last two albums Hero of the Grey Area and Wonderful Bite, giving them new life. The initial track Push Anyway is SGX''s hybrid remix/combination of two Wonderful Bite tracks, Die Anyway and Push. Prolific electronica producer Hollidayrain takes on Coactive for the second time and brings a chilled but heavy-hitting rendition, while Hy Bound''s Amity mix invokes some Trifonic-style grittied-up orchestral beats. Full of stellar multi-genre electronica tracks, SGXRMX3 is a fun journey back through previous SGX favorites.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/SGXRMX3/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/SGXRMX3/cover_200.jpg', 1329206400, 10),
(1285, 519, 'Synesthetic', 'SGX''s 2003 Synesthetic contains the seeds of what was to come of SGX''s style. Straddling several electronica genres, SGX experiments with different sounds and ideas while retaining his signature melodiousness and accessibility. The occasional odd-time meter slips into several tracks on the album, adding a unique flow to the grooves you won''t find in many electronica tracks.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/Synesthetic/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/Synesthetic/cover_200.jpg', 1318575600, 10),
(1286, 519, 'Wonderful Bite', 'Wonderful Bite is all about the sweet and the sour, the harsh and the soft mingling and merging for some of the slickest sounds you''ll hear (this week, this year, ever?).\n\nWB features a killer electro/breaks/hiphop track with dope rhymes by Navi and a clubby/synth-pop-esque tune vocals with vocals by Sekhret Hush.\n\nThe remaining instrumentals are killer SGX works ranging from Telefon Tel Aviv style natural chillness to talking analog synth badassery and beats ala The Crystal Method, all done up in SGX''s accessible, melodic, intelligent flavors. The tone is serious business plus a bit tongue in cheek.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/Wonderful Bite/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SGX/Wonderful Bite/cover_200.jpg', 1325923200, 8),
(1287, 520, 'Musings ballads and paradoxes', 'Put Beck, Tom Waits, Pink Floyd, and The Pixies on an operating table, inject a bolt of lightning, stitch up the scars, and the creature you get is Shadows From The Underground.\n\n"Musings, Ballads, and Paradoxes" is a collection of songs from three albums: "Love Songs For The Tortured Souls" (2007), "The Crooked Mile" (2008), and "Metamorphosis" (2009).\n\n"Love Songs For The Tortured Souls" is a journal of heartbreak, self-destruction, and forbidden love. It sums up my angst-ridden mid-20''s in Los Angeles. Writing and recording those songs helped me survive the darkness I was experiencing at the time.\n\nA lot changed for me after finishing "Love Songs." Within the course of a year, I was married and living in Colorado. I realized what my life could be like if I came to terms with the demons in my soul. "The Crooked Mile" is an exorcism of these demons. The songs document my turbulent journey on the road to inner peace.\n\nI started working on "Metamorphosis" immediately after I found out that I was going to become a father. The album is a narrative of my transition into pending fatherhood, with songs about commitment, sacrifice, and life-changes.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shadows from the Underground/Musings ballads and paradoxes/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shadows from the Underground/Musings ballads and paradoxes/cover_200.jpg', 1284188400, 3),
(1288, 520, 'Musings ballads and paradoxes (instrumental)', 'Put Beck, Tom Waits, Pink Floyd, and The Pixies on an operating table, inject a bolt of lightning, stitch up the scars, and the creature you get is "Shadows From The Underground".\n\n"Musings, Ballads, and Paradoxes" is a collection of songs from three albums: "Love Songs For The Tortured Souls" (2007), "The Crooked Mile" (2008), and "Metamorphosis" (2009).\n\n"Love Songs For The Tortured Souls" is a journal of heartbreak, self-destruction, and forbidden love. It sums up my angst-ridden mid-20''s in Los Angeles. Writing and recording those songs helped me survive the darkness I was experiencing at the time.\n\nA lot changed for me after finishing "Love Songs." Within the course of a year, I was married and living in Colorado. I realized what my life could be like if I came to terms with the demons in my soul. "The Crooked Mile" is an exorcism of these demons. The songs document my turbulent journey on the road to inner peace.\n\nI started working on "Metamorphosis" immediately after I found out that I was going to become a father. The album is a narrative of my transition into pending fatherhood, with songs about commitment, sacrifice, and life-changes.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shadows from the Underground/Musings ballads and paradoxes (instrumental)/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shadows from the Underground/Musings ballads and paradoxes (instrumental)/cover_200.jpg', 1313305200, 3),
(1289, 521, 'Equilibrium', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shane Jackman/Equilibrium/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shane Jackman/Equilibrium/cover_200.jpg', 1072080000, 4),
(1290, 521, 'Sanctuary', 'Featuring mellow rock-folk songs that are nevertheless replete with a certain sense of longing, Sanctuary shows why Shane Jackman is often called upon to represent the Southwest on stages all over the world. The subject matter and melodies are perfectly evocative of this region''s unique beauty, and Jackman''s emotive, lilting tenor has a multi-hued, painted-desert quality about it. An album about "gratitude, and the subtlety of things."\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shane Jackman/Sanctuary/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shane Jackman/Sanctuary/cover_200.jpg', 1138694400, 3),
(1291, 522, 'Echoes of Autumn', 'This is the second CD from Shawn Harris and a big musical departure from the first CD''s acoustic only journey. The music ranges from catchy rock all the way to Country, R ? B, Hard Rock Ballad and classically inspired songs and covers a broad spectrum of different styles along the way. "Echoes of Autumn" has many top notch studio musicians lending a hand and adding their unique style, including Magnatune artist Chris Juergensen and keeping things fresh while expanding the overall sound. From the opening track of "Sweet Charity" with its catchy pop style all the way to the haunting last track; "Let''s Roll" the emotional tribute to the 9/11 fallen. The music is fresh and the talent is inspired on every track creating a full body of musical experience along the way. This ones a keeper with a little something for everyone.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shawn Harris/Echoes of Autumn/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shawn Harris/Echoes of Autumn/cover_200.jpg', 1257580800, 6);
INSERT INTO `albums` (`id`, `artist_id`, `name`, `description`, `cover`, `cover_small`, `release_date`, `popularity`) VALUES
(1292, 522, 'Battle Call', 'From the first moments of the first song ("Can''t Let Go") with its lush Zoso guitars lilting in and out of a groove-heavy JPJ/Bonham backbeat, one thing is instantly clear: this isn''t a bunch of YouTube hopefuls fiddling around with Garage Band. These are real musicians at the top of their game. And they love the lost art of songwriting.\n\nWith Harris at the helm, those songs tell real stories. From the sweeping emotion of "Words To Say," to the sweet folky feel of "Only Love Can Bring," his voice radiates a warm mix of vulnerability, strength and passion all echoed equally in his lyrics. Like all master craftsmen, he understands that to know where you''re going, you''ve gotta know where you came from. It''s obvious he "gets" country, Celtic, blues, jazz and Randy Rhoads. That''s why his music takes you to other places. And that''s why his guitar playing is so brilliant.\n\nHe''s taken the spiritual journey that we''re all on and given it the perfect soundtrack.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shawn Harris/Battle Call/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shawn Harris/Battle Call/cover_200.jpg', 1345446000, 6),
(1293, 522, 'Temptation', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shawn Harris/Temptation/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shawn Harris/Temptation/cover_200.jpg', 1218178800, 7),
(1294, 523, 'She said save me', 'She Said Save Me''s debut self-titled album is a sincere, inspiring, and heart-warming experience straight from the heart of Canadian singer-songwriter Joe Vercillo. Recorded in the attic of a century home in Toronto, it''s raw and pure sound sticks to Vercillo''s life motto of keeping it simple and keeping it real. A mix between Acoustic, Folk, Punk, and Pop, all songs possess an extremely intimate, soothing and uplifting feel. And while most lyrics deal with the difficulties of life and relationships, there is always an underlying optimism and revelation of hope in each and every song. Vercillo''s main message in the album is that you can make anything into a good thing.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SHE SAID SAVE ME/She said save me/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SHE SAID SAVE ME/She said save me/cover_200.jpg', 1308985200, 12),
(1295, 524, 'Opium', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sherefe/Opium/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sherefe/Opium/cover_200.jpg', 1082876400, 14),
(1296, 524, 'Sala Sala', 'A full 78 minutes of Eastern Mediterranean soul food.\n\nGorgeous songs from Greece, Turkey, Egypt, Lebanon and Bulgaria. Native Tongues. Traditional instruments. Elegant and Danceable. Well honed from hundreds of performances at concerts, festivals, weddings and private parties, Sherefe''s rich sound is a melding of cello, bouzouki, oud, gadulka, saz, double bass, Middle Eastern percussion and evocative vocals (on 8 of the 12 songs).\n\nFeaturing James Hoskins, Jesse Manno, Zahara McLellan, Paul Brown and special guest vocalist Beth Quist, this album weaves a path through folk dance, belly dance and middle eastern classical styles.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sherefe/Sala Sala/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sherefe/Sala Sala/cover_200.jpg', 1312009200, 15),
(1297, 525, 'Transition', 'Transition is the collection of old and new songs. Primary it is not the real album, but a new demo for promotion purposes. So if somebody want to know how "Shino" sounds like, he/she should listen to this album.\n\nHowever, because of the much better quality of the sound, it is worthwhile for all the people, who already know my music to re-experience these songs. Because with Transition the music is a bit closer to the listener. Furthermore there are two new songs (Melancholia and Fall Days), one very old, but never recorded song (Schlaflied - Lullaby) and "My Mistress" as guitar version.\n\nTransition stands for passage, change, even mutation and metamorphosis. This album presents a mental process, where the human personality is searching for the light, explanations, sense and in this way it can learn the inner cleaning. Everything that i want to come alive with these 21 songs, is some kind of catharsis of modern age - the search for the very own but abstract I.\n\nThe order of the songs is chronological (not Schlaflied, it ought to be at the beginning) and some songs are much older than the project "Shino". In that way you can find the expression of my own search, my own catharsis, my own path in these songs.\n\nThanks to everybody, who supports me! Everybody who listens to my songs... Everybody who can feel and understand my songs... Everybody who plays and distributes my songs... I am not the great musician and song-maker, i just want to give you a bit of vitality and pureness, with all the perfection and rawness. In the ecstasy to find the best riff, the fastest solo, the groviest equipment and the professional appearance, our world of music became very plastic and dead.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shino/Transition/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shino/Transition/cover_200.jpg', 1345446000, 7),
(1298, 526, 'A Celebration of Robert Burns', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/A Celebration of Robert Burns/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/A Celebration of Robert Burns/cover_200.jpg', 1125903600, 28),
(1299, 526, 'The Almanac', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/The Almanac/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/The Almanac/cover_200.jpg', 1065682800, 32),
(1300, 526, 'The Castle of the Holly King', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/The Castle of the Holly King/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/The Castle of the Holly King/cover_200.jpg', 1065596400, 34),
(1301, 526, 'The Dawn of Joy', '"Chominciamento di gioia", or "The Dawn of Joy", is the title of a dance from a collection of medieval instrumental pieces in what is known (quite prosaically) as British Library, Additional 29987.\n\nThis manuscript, now in London, contains 119 compositions, all by composers of the Italian Trecento. Among the best known today are Francesco Landini, Niccolo da Perugia, and Jacopo da Bologna.\n\nThe instrumental works in the manuscript are grouped together and consist of several Istanpitte, Salterelli, and other dances. All have intriguing names, such as the title dance of our CD. Another memorable title, Principio de Virtu, might refer to a connection of the manuscript with the Visconti family in Milan: Gian Galeazzo Visconti was known as the Count of Virtu.\n\nThe Istanpitta, or Estampie, is the only medieval dance form for which descriptions as well as music have survived. Each phrase, or punctum, in an estampie is repeated. A refrain of differing length is inserted after each iteration of a phrase, alternating open and closed endings. The Salterello follows the same principle but tends to be shorter and less complicated in character.\n\nIt is quite extraordinary that these dances were written down at all, since we know that the players of these pieces, known as minstrels or jongleurs, mostly improvised their art. Successful minstrels needed a tinker''s bagful of talents. According to Guiraut de Calancon (Fadet Joglar), they were expected to play at least nine instruments, have a knack for storytelling and songwriting, sing, improvise dances, juggle, perform magic tricks, demonstrate acrobatic skills, catch little apples on knives, imitate birdsong, jump through four hoops, and, if possible, dance with bears!\n\nAlthough playing for dancers would have been an important part of the professional musicians'' job, they didn''t hesitate to play vocal works. They made their own arrangements or improvised on songs their audiences knew. Even in church this practice was not unknown: the famous hymn Ave maris stella served as the base for a highly ornamented version in the Faenza Codex, a 15th-century manuscript containing instrumental arrangements of mostly 14th-century vocal polyphony.\n\nWomen played instruments, as amateurs and as professionals. Francesco da Barberino, in his Reggimento e costumi di donna, a 14th-c. treatise on the education of young women, states that they should learn to play instruments, especially the vielle and the harp; they should know how to sing and compose text and music and be able to perform these songs, accompanying themselves. In Boccaccio''s Decameron female musicians outnumber the male players.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/The Dawn of Joy/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/The Dawn of Joy/cover_200.jpg', 1333350000, 29),
(1302, 526, 'Mistral', 'Known for her strong, clear, powerful playing of the vielle (medieval violin, aka a "fiddle") Shira Kammen offers up a particularly vibrant collection of her work with this release of medieval songs from France, Italy, and Brittany. The album is named after the bitingly cold winter wind that flows from the Alps down to the Mediterranean coast, but the performances are all bright, joyous, and warm. Full liner notes available with download!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/Mistral/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/Mistral/cover_200.jpg', 1145084400, 34),
(1303, 526, 'Ragged, Rent and Torn', 'John writes: My first exposure to Shira Kammen was during an all-day Medieval music festival in Berkeley, California, where she was doing an hourlong satire of early music entitled "La Foolia". I particularly remember Dowland''s renaissance-smash-hit "Flow my Tears" performed on four accordeons. Shira plays the Vielle, which is a precursor to the violin, and it''s where we get the word "fiddle" from. She a big deal in medieval circles, having been in many of the major groups that play this repertoire (Ensemble Alcatraz, Project Ars Nova, Sequentia, Hesperion XX and Boston Camerata). In "Ragged, Rent and Torn", Shira and friends turn their attention to the bawdy, fun and lewd songs of the period. The artistry is definitely taken seriously, but at the same time this is an album of music "just for fun".\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/Ragged, Rent and Torn/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/Ragged, Rent and Torn/cover_200.jpg', 1236412800, 35),
(1304, 526, 'Music of Waters', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/Music of Waters/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shira Kammen/Music of Waters/cover_200.jpg', 1065682800, 47),
(1305, 527, 'broken vow', 'Brutal, down to earth thrash-metal with a good portion of progressive influences.\n\nThis album bleeds together our individual musical backgrounds of metal music, permutating the different stylistic serene, melodic, thrashing, raving mad elements we hold so dear.\n\nBy the sweat of our brows we were working by day and making music during night shifts, from composition, arrangements, lyrics, recording, production, mastering and artwork. Everything done by ourselves, anything you name it. The variety of broken vow reflects our friendship and independence we preserved over all the years.\n\nOur lifeblood is seeping through the songs. The end of life for some, a life source for others, so much came about, still so many deeds undone. At long last our music we like to share with all our friends and loved ones, on this and on the other side of life.\n\n..no vow broken as much as the sacred oath of love..\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/sHIT for bRAINS/broken vow/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/sHIT for bRAINS/broken vow/cover_200.jpg', 1332054000, 3),
(1306, 528, 'Ethnic', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shiva in Exile/Ethnic/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shiva in Exile/Ethnic/cover_200.jpg', 1064473200, 28),
(1307, 528, 'Nour', 'We are taken on a journey of revolution, of change, understanding and ultimately into the deep mysteries of the east. In tracks like Viva La Revolucion, We''re All One and He''neya we are thrown into the middle of the conflicts as Yana leads the way in protest. From there we are taken on a journey of spiritual understanding throughout the east. In Anubis your mind will dance within the ancient temples of Egypt, you will take your spiritual journey further with tracks such as Desert of Yunus and Blue Healing. There are songs to make you dance, to make you cry, to have you stand up and scream to the skies... why!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shiva in Exile/Nour/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Shiva in Exile/Nour/cover_200.jpg', 1215586800, 27),
(1308, 529, 'Music from Braid', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sieber, Kammen and Fulton/Music from Braid/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sieber, Kammen and Fulton/Music from Braid/cover_200.jpg', 1239260400, 63),
(1309, 530, 'India meets Persia', 'On India meets Persia Simone Angele and Amir Kalhor take the listener on an intricate musical journey. The artists explore the classical styles of India and Iran, using the traditional instruments of the respective countries: the Indian Sitar and the Persian Tar.\n\nThe artists strive towards unity and come together in the two duets "India meets Persia" (track no. 2) and "Persia meets India" (track no. 5), where the two styles converge like two voices from different lands, each speaking with its own accent. The pieces "Kirvan" (track no. 1), "Isfahan" (track no. 3), "Solitidi" (track no. 4), and "Joys of Spring" (track no. 6) represent original Indian and Persian classical styles. "Sunny Days" (track no. 7), a pop piece with vocals and sitar, ends the musical journey and takes the listeners back to the west on a light note.\n\nBabua Pahari is the percussive force on Tabla and accompanies Simone Angele in the Indian pieces.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Simone Angele and Amir Kalhor/India meets Persia/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Simone Angele and Amir Kalhor/India meets Persia/cover_200.jpg', 1334386800, 21),
(1310, 531, 'A Dream Play', 'S.J. Pettersson''s second classical release is an unorthodox opera (with as many instrumental tracks as vocal ones) based on the famous 1901 stage play "A Dream Play" by August Strindberg, one of Strindberg''s most admired and influential dramas, seen as an important precursor to both dramatic expressionism and surrealism.\n\nStrindberg himself describes the play this way in his famous preface: "Everything can happen; everything is possible and likely to happen. Time and space do not exist; on an insignificant foundation of reality the imagination spills out and weaves new patterns - a concoction of memories, experiences, improbabilities and utterly free improvisations. The characters are split in twain, duplicate, multiply and evaporate. They condense, distill and again reunite. But one mind rises above them all. It is the Dreamer; for him there are no secrets, no consistencies, no scruples, no Law. He does not judge, nor gives out pardons but impartially relates the content of the dream itself. And as the dream often painful is, and much less often joyfully free-spirited, there is a tone of sadness and empathy with everything alive in this labyrinthine tale now beginning to unfold."\n\nThe primary character in the play is Agnes, a daughter of the Vedic god Indra. She descends to Earth to bear witness to problems of human beings. She meets about 40 characters, some of them having a clearly symbolical value (such as four deans representing theology, philosophy, medicine, and law). After deciding to experience human life first hand by becoming a human, she experiences all sorts of human suffering such as poverty, cruelty, and the often difficult routine of family life. She marries and has a child but in the end the daughter of gods realizes that human beings are to be pitied and is faced with heartbreaking choice: to return to the Heavens in an attempt to persuade her Father Indra to intervene and ease the suffering of humanity, or to stay on Earth and care for her earthly child. Finally, she returns to the Heaven and this moment corresponds to the awakening from a dream-like sequence of events.\n\nS.J. Pettersson''s treatment of the play is to basically tell the story, or often, as is the case with the purely instrumental pieces, through emotions. The scenes are replaced with lyrical narratives, often in first person, and characters just mentioned in passing, comes alive with their own tales. There is no attempt to re-tell the story (which in itself is as difficult to follow and make sense out of as indeed our dreams are) but rather to express it in another medium in the terms of that medium''s capability to reach where the written word is not able to penetrate. The result is almost closer to a song cycle (using 4 different vocal soloists that are all as similar as they are different in character, much like the shifting characters of the play) and with powerful instrumental pieces that in an almost hyper-romantic fashion carry the narrative along with their own emotional contents. The musical style is highly anachronistic but the perhaps biggest influence seems to be Weill''s German operas from the 1920s blended with elements of Bach, Shubert, Chopin, Prokofiev''s lyrical side, even Shostakovich at time. It is a very accessible work often hiding its harmonic and rhythmical complexity below the surface. The performances throughout are stellar with many of Los Angeles top classical musicians lending their hands and, seemingly, their hearts. The vocal performances are highly unusual and emotionally effective, often sung in pure tone avoiding vibrato and with an approach to communicate the often difficult scenes without resorting to obvious vocal tricks and audience pandering. As a result, the suffering seems to be located internally rather than externally which in many ways increases the personal aspect of what is a universal condition.\n\nThe libretto is poetically very beautiful and in the end leaves the play behind with a final piece, Hope, that seems to transfer the words and thoughts from that of Strindberg to the composers own mouth.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SJ Pettersson/A Dream Play/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SJ Pettersson/A Dream Play/cover_200.jpg', 1314428400, 12),
(1311, 531, 'Romeo and Juliet', 'Romeo and Juliet is the second ballet from Swedish born composer S.J. Pettersson.\n\nIt is written and performed for and by a chamber orchestra, albeit with some odd instrumentation thrown in for good measure.\n\nHis approach is as usual very poly-stylistic with an oboe melody opening the Prologue as a nod to Tchaikovsky and a violin passage in the elegy like movement that conjures up Prokofiev. Then we hear Bartok-like aggressive pieces mixed with hyper romantic melodies reminiscent of Satie set in a post minimalist soundscape that in spite of the varied ingredients still manages to retain a firm consistency as a whole.\n\nHis approach to telling the story progresses "backwards" with the death scene of the two Star Cross''d lovers opening the ballet and the rest of the story being told as a flashback focusing almost exclusively on the two main characters. All of the titles are culled from the play by Shakespeare and the people knowledgeable with the play will have no problems putting them in context of the story.\n\nThe instrumental (and vocal) performances are nothing short of heartbreaking and the work is performed by some of the finest classical musicians around today including Yana Reznik on the piano and Maksim Velichkin on cello just to name two. It is truly a remarkable and magic journey that concludes triumphantly as an homage to love, rather than the tragedy of two lovers unintended suicide.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SJ Pettersson/Romeo and Juliet/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SJ Pettersson/Romeo and Juliet/cover_200.jpg', 1323676800, 5),
(1312, 532, 'Litha', 'Skarazula''s second album is the continuity of the pagan rituals theme. After Ostara, their first release, comes Litha, the summer solstice festival in the Saxon culture. Litha includes songs from many parts of the ancient world; there''s a troubadour''s pastourelle, a sephardic love song, a Cantiga de Santa Maria, Turkish, Breton and Italian music. Take a trip around the world while enjoying both the original and traditional pieces of Litha. The music is played on instruments built by two of the Skarazula''s members which give Litha its unique sound.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skarazula/Litha/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skarazula/Litha/cover_200.jpg', 1307775600, 43),
(1313, 532, 'Ostara', 'Half of the music on Ostara comes from medieval Europe between the 13th and 16th century and the other half are originals inspired by this great era composed by two members of Skarazula. All of the stringed instruments that you hear on the album were made by the luthier of the group except the psaltery, which was made by the percussionist, and the ud which comes from Turkey.\n\nOstara is Skarazula''s first album and is an homage to the pagan spring equinox festivities of ancient times. The name comes from the Teutonic spring goddess "Eastre", that later became Easter in English.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skarazula/Ostara/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skarazula/Ostara/cover_200.jpg', 1303542000, 29),
(1314, 533, 'The Secret Life of Angels', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SkinMechanix/The Secret Life of Angels/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SkinMechanix/The Secret Life of Angels/cover_200.jpg', 1196409600, 9),
(1315, 534, 'Babblesick', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skitzo/Babblesick/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skitzo/Babblesick/cover_200.jpg', 1064732400, 4),
(1316, 534, 'Heavy Shit', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skitzo/Heavy Shit/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skitzo/Heavy Shit/cover_200.jpg', 1125903600, 3),
(1317, 534, 'Hellavator Musick', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skitzo/Hellavator Musick/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skitzo/Hellavator Musick/cover_200.jpg', 1064905200, 3),
(1318, 534, 'Synusar''sukus', 'Synusar''sukus ( Sign us or Suck Us) 1994 full length CD.\n\nSkitzo has been making recordings since 1981 but this is a special new recording of Synusar''sukus, the original CDs have long sold out and are out of print. This version has been re-mastered and beefed up in volume. The band, management and roadies all collaborated on this CD so it was a total group empire release.\n\nSynusar''sukus is poignantly special as it includes a golden touch from the band''s lead guitar player, Ken Springer, who masterfully played on this iconic metal release. This was Ken''s last album. He died in 1995 from a motorcycle accident in Northern California. This incarnation of music and musicians quickly disburse and this era has ended. The next few albums that Lance Ozanix wrote and recorded were virtually solo having only mannequins on stage with him playing to a track show.\n\nThe album on Magnatune is a compilation called Babble-Sick best of release / from the two CDs Psychobabble and Got Sick. This CD is dedicated to the people who put work into it. The band was: Ken Springer - guitar/vocals , Lance Ozanix - bass/vocals, Scott Reynolds drums/vocals and Stacy Maisenbach (Bassy Stacy/DJ Skully) : Kurt Houser, Kathy Kingsley, and Joe Berelli producers.\n\nLook for the video online of Tool Box Murders shot by Hogwyld Films with Jeff Johnson as the director. The band played with many national acts this tour: Machine Head, D.R.I., Gwar, Mentors, Malevolent Creation, Epidemic and Jim Martin from Faith No More.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skitzo/Synusar''sukus/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Skitzo/Synusar''sukus/cover_200.jpg', 1330243200, 3),
(1319, 535, 'Slimstrumentals Vol 1', 'Specializing in Hip Hop, RnB, Electronica and Nu Jazz composing/beat making, David "Slimdog" Bell Jr. is the founder of Slimdog Productions. Also a member of the award winning Indie Hip Hop group, Block Scholars, David is trying to bring his music production to the level that the group he belongs to has achieved. It starts with his first album of instrumental compositions of many styles called, "Slimstrumentals Vol. I".\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Slimdog Productions/Slimstrumentals Vol 1/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Slimdog Productions/Slimstrumentals Vol 1/cover_200.jpg', 1343545200, 10),
(1320, 536, 'Tides EP', 'Water. Tides. The ocean slowly and imperceptibly coming again and again to the shore, bringing light waves over time. Welcome to the new Snowsleep ambient composition.\n\nTides consists of three parts, the three tides. Each of these parts is somewhat similar to the other and at the same time has its own personality. In one part you can hear elements of Post-Rock, another the sound of Pink Floyd''s 80s period and in the third audible elements of modern electronic music.\n\nThroughout the listener experiences a warm sound, and as Tides finally ends it leaves us to contemplate the distant expanses of the ocean and the hope that we will once again return.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Snowsleep/Tides EP/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Snowsleep/Tides EP/cover_200.jpg', 1423382400, 83),
(1321, 537, 'Ahsas', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Ahsas/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Ahsas/cover_200.jpg', 1053068400, 29),
(1322, 537, 'Balance', 'Solace, a.k.a. Jeremiah M. Soto, has earned renown as producer thtrough several best-selling ambient, world, and electronica album, including the Magnatune favorite Ahsas. This time Soto takes us deeper into his imagined east by fusing his dynamic Middle Eastern rhythms with exotic Far Eastern instrumentation and percussion. Few people could blend disparate cultures and eras so successfully, but Soto''s impeccable studio craftsmanship and knowledge of multiple genres enable him to do just that . Taiko drums, guqin, and pan pipe meet with subtle trip hop and Middle Eastern beats to form a musical vision out of time and place-an entirely original and idyllic meeting of two worlds. Be sure to check out the stunning vocals by Kate St. Pierre on "Haiku" and "Kimono Mojo."\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Balance/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Balance/cover_200.jpg', 1126249200, 19),
(1323, 537, 'The Gathering Season', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/The Gathering Season/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/The Gathering Season/cover_200.jpg', 1058166000, 17),
(1324, 537, 'Iman', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Iman/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Iman/cover_200.jpg', 1058511600, 16),
(1325, 537, 'The Medusa Crown', 'With the opening song resembling a beautiful nightmare, Solace''s journey into the minds innermost secrets and insecurities, reveals a subtle musical edge borrowing on industrial and IDM genres. This prologue gives us a peek into the structural dynamic and experimental edge to Solace''s forth coming CD, "Gorgon Days". Don''t let the subtleties fool you. There is no way to describe Solace''s latest release, just listen.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/The Medusa Crown/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/The Medusa Crown/cover_200.jpg', 1257580800, 12),
(1326, 537, 'Moon Moth Mixes', 'When artists get remixed this well, it''s not only because there''s a demand for more of their work--it''s because their work inspires a multitude of approaches to the music. Bringing together everything that is mysterious and seductive in both electronic and Middle Eastern music, the Moon Moth Mixes are seriously great. Deep, dark, and very sexy, this is the kind of album you can only make when you have achieved fierce levels of expertise and fearlessness in your craft.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Moon Moth Mixes/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Moon Moth Mixes/cover_200.jpg', 1192690800, 14),
(1327, 537, 'Nagari', 'The most mysterious and complex album that Solace has released to date, Nagari is a seductive exploration of the darker side of Middle Eastern music. Featuring serpentine vocals of Kate St. Pierre, masterfully detailed percussion of Ruben Von Rompaey, and pristine instrumentation by Tim Rayborn and J.M. Soto, this album seems a particularly princely jewel in the already very bedecked Solace crown. The sound design and production are downright superlative. We particularly love the dynamic breaks of "Sorrow and Veil" as well as the modern seduction of the title track, "Nagari," but the best track on the album by far is the undulating "Condemnation," a track resplendent in sexiness and self-control. If you''re a patient enough audiophile to snag the WAV files for this album, you''re in for a treat when you listen to "Pharisees Prayer" on headphones. We could say this album features a blend of acoustic and electronic instrumentation, but the two are so seamlessly joined together, you''ll hardly notice. Rather, you''ll hear a kind of musical path beng laid out for you, one that takes you deep into forbidden, ancient places as well as strange new territories.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Nagari/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Nagari/cover_200.jpg', 1193814000, 15),
(1328, 537, 'Opium Head', 'Lose yourself in the intoxicating sounds of OPIUM HEAD.\n\nThis aural narcotic is an experiment in structure and sound, designed without regard for genre. The songs contained herein are not compositions, but rather arrangements of various Indian Bhangra and Bollywood samples demodulated from their organic forms, modified, then arranged in a rhythmic/melodic mix. The parameter prescribed for this project was to create an organic mix laced with minimal electronica, as compared to an over-the-counter electronic mix dosed with minimal organic sounds.\n\nMusic is our drug, take a listen and join us in our addiction.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Opium Head/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Opium Head/cover_200.jpg', 1262937600, 24),
(1329, 537, 'Rhythm Of The Dance', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Rhythm Of The Dance/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Rhythm Of The Dance/cover_200.jpg', 1058511600, 22),
(1330, 537, 'Satya', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Satya/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Satya/cover_200.jpg', 1058511600, 16),
(1331, 537, 'ShaWaza', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/ShaWaza/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/ShaWaza/cover_200.jpg', 1052809200, 13),
(1332, 537, 'Vadalna', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Vadalna/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solace/Vadalna/cover_200.jpg', 1058166000, 15),
(1333, 538, 'Flare', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solar Cycle/Flare/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solar Cycle/Flare/cover_200.jpg', 1228118400, 9),
(1334, 538, 'Silver Lights', 'Best selling Magnatune artist DJ Markitos delivers another ultra-sophisticated cocktail of downtempo house, electronica and ambient styles. Ten silvery sleek tracks have style to burn and the title tune, "Silver Lights" is so gorgeous and sexy that it makes you feel like you''re driving your Alfa Romeo up the coastline at midnight. Actually, the whole album does. Check it out.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solar Cycle/Silver Lights/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solar Cycle/Silver Lights/cover_200.jpg', 1196409600, 7),
(1335, 538, 'Sunlight', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solar Cycle/Sunlight/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Solar Cycle/Sunlight/cover_200.jpg', 1125903600, 7),
(1336, 539, 'Du Deluge Au Desert', '"Du deluge au desert" is a voyage into a surreal landscape of sounds, with climate change used as an underlying theme.\n\nOn his second album, Soleil Vert mixes electronic sounds with the ambient sounds of nature, painting a surrealist picture of planet Earth.\n\nOpening with a bestiary of living creatures, like crude digital memories of a paradise lost for ever... The album then brings you underwater with "Sous la mer...", and up with the areal beats of "Ozone"... the voyages continues with the epic "Du deluge au desert" then the journey ends up in a desolate place, like in a post-apocaliptic future on "Planete desolation"... You can bet that the listener will have made quite a journey... from a deluge to a desert.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Soleil Vert/Du Deluge Au Desert/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Soleil Vert/Du Deluge Au Desert/cover_200.jpg', 1278745200, 5),
(1337, 539, 'Genetiquement modifie', 'As a concept album about genetically modified food, "Genetiquement modifie" is made of interrupted sequences, spliced fragments, inspired by the techniques used for the modification of living organisms, at the cellular level, using foreign DNA... all transposed into sound manipulation and textures.\n\nHeavy bass pedal notes under harsh percussion and flickering synths. Tangerinesque monosynth solos over a minimal tapestry... Harsh glitchy rhythms, raspy bass lines, minimalists sequences, this album is a departure from the techniques Soleil Vert used before... This is Soleil Vert, but Genetically Modified!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Soleil Vert/Genetiquement modifie/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Soleil Vert/Genetiquement modifie/cover_200.jpg', 1273820400, 4),
(1338, 539, 'L ere du petrole', 'Using our current era of fossil fuel usage as an inspiration, "L''ere du petrole" fits the bill of a concept album.\n\nThe synthetic is not far from the organic as petrol becomes plastic, and this album share this quality. From jungle atmospheres to the glassy and futuristic vision of "Cite Utopie", Soleil Vert delivers a texture-rich album driven by a strong emotional feel.\n\nIn line with Soleil Vert previous album release, "Du deluge au desert", L''ere du petrole" is bound to appeal to a surprising array of open-minded listeners, from fans of ambient/IDM genres to those coming from psychedelic and space-rock references.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Soleil Vert/L ere du petrole/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Soleil Vert/L ere du petrole/cover_200.jpg', 1287817200, 4),
(1339, 540, 'Trancelucent', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Somadrone/Trancelucent/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Somadrone/Trancelucent/cover_200.jpg', 1069142400, 4),
(1340, 541, 'Evil', 'Evil is the second hard rock album of Sophia Marie. Also released under her live band name of ''Wicked Wench'', the goal with Evil was to get a more raw, hard hitting sound than her first album, ''Twisted'', without losing any of the high quality production.\n\nThough Sophia has written songs in many different genres, she uses Rock to express the most intense emotions. ''Rescue Me'' is a classic power ballad; ''Underneath it All'' a rage against people who disappoint you in life; ''Circle of Lies'' about the screaming souls a loveless couple; ''Fear is the Enemy'' an inspirational motivator and ''Winter Kiss'' a Snow White fantasy duet with Male singer Clayton Mitchell.\n\nSophia also continues as she did on her first album to try and honestly express female sexuality, without falling into the contrived titillation of modern pop music, with ''Take Me'', ''The Swinger'' and ''No Escape''. She sends a message out to those who may pass judgement with the anthemic song ''Wicked Angel''. Originally this was to be the title of the album, but late inclusion of the song ''You Are Evil'', induced a change, which, it is hoped, will produce a little thought about what ''Evil'' actually is.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sophia Marie/Evil/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sophia Marie/Evil/cover_200.jpg', 1391673600, 30),
(1341, 541, 'Twisted', 'The Twisted album is structured around the theme of love and loss, the songs carefully arranged to create a loose story or old style concept album. The power and intensity of the songs in the centre of the album suggest Sophia Marie has drawn on some personal experiences to create songs that should drive deep into the all but the coldest of hearts. The cheeky and sometimes downright sinful content of the rest of the songs suggest her previous out of control lifestyle has not been entirely forgotten!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sophia Marie/Twisted/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sophia Marie/Twisted/cover_200.jpg', 1269586800, 15),
(1342, 542, 'Heartwood', 'With its uniquely ancient to modern sound, Sora''s music weaves together contemporary rock with the mystical moods of Celtic folk, medieval madrigals, and other modes of early music. The result is a heady, colorfully evocative tapestry that conjures the imagery of long ago but feels just as current as anything from the present musical landscape all the while sounding like little else on today''s airwaves. Across the album''s moody and atmospheric tracks Sora''s heartrending soprano glides like a graceful dove through swirling mists of violin, piano, harp, synthesizer, cello, guitar, percussion, and other instruments to deliver sweeping performances. The music moves from lightness of thought and sound to richly dense soundscapes. The music is not easy to categorize, but ultimately it is about the journeys we make within ourselves.\n\nIt feels at times as if music comes not so much from within me but that is passes through me in the ways of half-remembered dreams. Yet, I feel we each are a filter to the heart of the world, we shape it, to some extent with our own experiences, perceptions and feelings. I like to think of each of these songs as filtered through my own heart, as water passes through a teabag, colored with the pieces of me. The music itself washes through me, as wind passes through the trees, but in doing so is imprinted by my spirit as it exits through my voice, my fingers, through the notes that I write. The songs are not about me, but I cannot deny that I find myself in them.\n\nIt is my belief that we are influenced by the environments in which we live. The outer world interacts with the inner, affecting thought patters, creation and consciousness. Found within the pages here are objects from my environment; that which creates the spaces I inhabit musically, emotionally, spiritually and physically. Leaves, petals, rocks, crystal; these are the heartgifts I wish to share with you.\n\nHeartwood is the core, it is the truths we carry, it is the place of secret whisperings, it is our beginnings and endings. It is the axis upon which our lives spin. It is my most humble hope that perhaps as these songs pass through you, that you will find something of yourself within them as well.\n\nHeartwood is an examination of the places the heart inhabits.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sora/Heartwood/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sora/Heartwood/cover_200.jpg', 1351407600, 17),
(1343, 542, 'Scorpion Moon', 'Ethereal folk influenced vocals soar over lush world music instrumentation.\n\nSora delves deeply into stories, both mythic and modern, that transcend time and place, boldly claiming the complexity of contradictory truths within the emotional landscapes that shape our own personal stories.\n\nLight and dark, joy and sorrow, urban versus nature, birth/rebirth but also death, ancient and modern, Sora delicately balances it all to provide music that is at once ethereal and grounded. This evocative music fuses the poetry of the Celts, the global influence of World and the stunning melodiousness of Neo-Classical into music that defies the boundaries of traditional genre categorization. Listen and find your mythic self.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sora/Scorpion Moon/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sora/Scorpion Moon/cover_200.jpg', 1376895600, 20),
(1344, 543, 'In Spite of it All', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SoulPrint/In Spite of it All/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SoulPrint/In Spite of it All/cover_200.jpg', 1076140800, 3),
(1345, 544, 'Sound of Seventy Three', 'With one eye on the history of recorded music and the other on its future, Sound of Seventy Three''s self-titled debut is an experiment in technology, economy, and fidelity that results in a genre-jumping album which still seamlessly comes together as a whole.\n\nBridging the gap between Post-Rock and Electronica, Sound of Seventy Three manage to find the commonality between the two genres placing long-form post-rock right next to trip-hop influenced remixes without either sounding out of place. The original tracks are often guitar-driven instrumental pieces in the best post-rock tradition making full use of the electric guitars'' immense range to craft dynamic-cinematic music that demands to be listened to. The remixes on the other hand explore the sound of recoded media as a subtle form of musical expression with noise like tape hiss, vinyl dust, and digital artifacts being intentionally added to the music to add ''color'' and ''shade'' to the remix of each track.\n\nInspired as much by the ''Lomo'' toy-photography movement, as by ''gadget-music'', Sound of Seventy Three also explore the musical expressiveness of what many would consider ''toy'' instruments. Some of these instruments include the Stylophone, Stylophone Beatbox, Korg Kaossilator and Vox AmPlugs. Amazingly the resulting music is neither comedic, campy, or kitschy and instead has a gravity and intimacy that proves that you can make ''real'' music with these new affordable musical tools. Just like your favorite mix-tape Sound of Seventy Three is a collection of music that defies easy categorization and yet is all the better for it.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sound of Seventy Three/Sound of Seventy Three/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sound of Seventy Three/Sound of Seventy Three/cover_200.jpg', 1279350000, 9),
(1346, 544, 'The Way we Feel', '"The Way We Feel" is a return to the music that gave birth to unique retro/rock/lounge genre that is Sound of Seventy Three.\n\nNearly half the album: A Farewell; The Great Rest; Ahh, Tow Bots; and Spilt Milk are songs that were gigged with the live-band back when Sound of Seventy Three were playing shows in the Atlanta area in the mid to late 00s as a two-guitar, bass and drums quartet. Some of the material on the album even dates all the way back to 2004. The other half of the album are mostly tunes that were written or started at that time but never finished or incorporated into the live sets. Only one song ''Jyuu'' is a completely new composition which also has the distinction of being the longest track on the album.\n\nThe result is an album that is sure to please old fans of the live-band as well as newer fans who have fallen in love with So73''s guitars-meet-gadgets approach to the last album. Unlike the first Sound of Seventy Three album which was essentially a collection of singles, the songs on this album were chosen specifically to fit together well stylistically but also to take the listener on a sonic journey when listened to together.\n\nThe album opens with ''Shields'' (dedicated to Kevin Shields of My Bloody Valentine) which is rock minimalism at its finest. The whole song being a simple three note melody on one chord that slowly builds to a noise-filled shoegaze nirvana. The volume and the noise level drop with the restrained yet intense ''A Farewell'' which features Doug''s distinctive use of the Ebow - an electronic bow that allows you to sustain a note on the guitar indefinitely. However things quickly ramp up again with ''Variations'' which has a one-note bass groove that gives it an incessant almost bolero-like quality.\n\nThis is followed by the loud-then-soft-then-loud-again dynamics of ''The Great Rest'' where the melody is carried by the bass-guitar; a distinguishing feature of much of Sound of Seventy Three''s music. Next come two tracks that build gradually, each more intensely than the last. ''The First Waltz'' starts with some gentle arpeggios and lap-steel volume-swells that slowly build until finally it climaxes with tremolo picked guitar, lap-steel, and Stylophone all playing the melody in unison. ''You Say Today, I Say Tomorrow'' opens with a chiming guitar riff and beautiful melody to slowly build instrument by instrument, pedal by pedal, to an almost orchestral crescendo at the end. Things get calm and quiet again with ''Jyuu'' both the longest track on the album and also the most ambient. This is followed by two tracks that again highlight Sound of Seventy Three''s distinctive use of the bass-guitar. ''Ahh, Tow Bots'' - originally titled ''Autobots'' - is an old favorite from the band''s live-set and features bass arpeggios played on an Agile Harm 1 Extended Scale Guitar - A Fender Bass VI style instrument that resembles cross between a bass and a baritone guitar. The track ends with that same bass playing full-on distorted chords toward the end. ''Spilt Milk'' has the bass again handling the melody and sees a return of Doug''s distinctive use of the Ebow to add interest and tension to the melody. Finally the album ends with the gentle and relaxed ''The Way We Feel'' where guitar and six string bass double one another playing chord-melody as a delayed Kaossilator adds a gentle rhythmic bed underneath.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sound of Seventy Three/The Way we Feel/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sound of Seventy Three/The Way we Feel/cover_200.jpg', 1299830400, 11),
(1347, 545, 'ILL-Emental', 'Deeply rooted high energy electronic dance music. A musician, martial artist, dancer, and aerialist Jordan has an innate need to express the strange and mystifying tunes that populate his head and watch as they become energetic and tangible movement on the dance floor.\n\nAbout the pieces on this album:\n\nWind - gliding along like a leaf getting blown by the wind.\nWater - Triumphant journey that starts on the calm shores, ends up in the stormy waters and dives into the depths of the sea.\nElectricity - high energy, melodic dubstep with spastic basslines.\nEarth - bone crushing dubstep that will melt your face.\nMachine - mechanical destruction and computerized melody.\nFire - explosive and action packed coupling dubstep and ghetto hip hop.\nPain Reliever - Epic hip hop that fuses Hans Zimmer, dirty basslines, haunting guitar melodies and floor stomping beats.\nAerial fantasy - drum and bass with a beautiful chords and smooth guitar licks.\nLeave you there - funky retro disco house\nDreams dont sleep - sexy and smooth rnb to ease your mnd and calm your soul.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Soundami/ILL-Emental/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Soundami/ILL-Emental/cover_200.jpg', 1356854400, 4),
(1348, 546, 'Autophile', 'A best selling genre album on Magnatune, Autophile feature''s Spinecar''s darkly precise yet deeply cathartic hard rock/metal, built on the the new and interesting structures that they''ve proven so adept at bringing in to their music. With its cascading, yet tightly controled fury, "Home" is the culmination of everything they''ve done so far, and "Soul Patch" is another great example of how experimental they can get with structure, without ever losing interest. Their work remains tight and cohesive no matter where they go with it. Most importantly, really good metal has to have both real darkness and real musicianship, and this album has both of these in spades. Eric Braymer and Larry Gagliardi sound genuinely wrathful in a way that surely gratifies the metal gods, while drummer Art Bernstein keeps it all in the pocket with a masterful sense of timing.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spinecar/Autophile/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spinecar/Autophile/cover_200.jpg', 1064214000, 5),
(1349, 546, 'Passive Aggressive', 'A handful of Spinecar''s rare recordings surface on Passive Aggressive, a collection of some of their more obscure, previously unreleased material. We love Eric Braymer''s vaguely Celtic guitar line on "Into You," and are thrilled that Art Bernstein was so into it that he stepped away from his typically more restrained/minimalistic style to get really medieval with that one. The cathartic "Release" has that really interesting, sine-wave structure that so defines much of Spinecar''s music--and both "True" and "Backstabber" are Spinecar doing what we think they do best--album rocking!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spinecar/Passive Aggressive/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spinecar/Passive Aggressive/cover_200.jpg', 1064214000, 4),
(1350, 546, 'Up from the mud', 'Like the very best album rock of the 1970''s, Up From the Mud by New York metal trio Spinecar explores a lot of what''s making metal so interesting lately. Asymmetric grooves stay low to the ground while Eric Braymer''s vocals alternate between soaring and searing. The musicianship here is exceptionally strong throughout and we''re especially fans of drummer Art Bernstein''s unexpected rhythms and minimailist approach to metal drumming. Recommended tracks include "Waste Away," the fantastic album rocker "Smoke" and "KC Ripoff." "Leech" is a bit of a miss, but everything else here is great. Check it out!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spinecar/Up from the mud/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spinecar/Up from the mud/cover_200.jpg', 1064214000, 6);
INSERT INTO `albums` (`id`, `artist_id`, `name`, `description`, `cover`, `cover_small`, `release_date`, `popularity`) VALUES
(1351, 547, 'Unexpected Guest at a Cancelled Party', 'A new, long awaited album has finally hit the streets. Aptly entitled "Unexpected Guest At A Cancelled Party", the album is packed with previously unreleased recordings and concert favourites from the vintage 1982 - 1985 years.\n\nListen to Spoons ... again, or for the first time ... and join in the retro 80s fun.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spoons/Unexpected Guest at a Cancelled Party/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spoons/Unexpected Guest at a Cancelled Party/cover_200.jpg', 1265356800, 24),
(1352, 547, 'Static in Transmission', 'A unique brand of upbeat, danceable music that quickly become anthems. Gold. Who could have predicted the magnitude of a phenomenon known as "retro 80s"?\n\nThe songs were written in a six-month period in 2010 and roughly in the order they appear on the album. The album was recorded at Producer Jeff Carter''s studio in Guelph Ontario. The music video for the first single "You Light Up" was filmed in Toronto Ontario. The cover for the CD features a picture of Sandy Horne and Gordon Deppe and numerous symbols and signs including morse code, circuit diagrams, and even hieroglyphics. Fans who purchased the album were challenged to find secret codes that would unlock two additional bonus downloads on the band''s official website.\n\nStatic in Transmission is the 8th studio album by the Canadian New Wave band Spoons. Released in early 2011 on CD and iTunes it is their first album of new material in 23 years since 1988''s Vertigo Tango.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spoons/Static in Transmission/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Spoons/Static in Transmission/cover_200.jpg', 1416729600, 74),
(1353, 548, 'The Art of Analog Diversion', 'WIth its lovely circular structures and space-y synths, The Art of Analog Diversion is a pared down ambient album that works just as well in the background as it does in the fore. Faithful analog and digital synths are featured alongside vintage drum machines, and together these sounds form patterns that morph and take on different sonic shapes as each track progresses. On this album, Bobby DeVito has found the exact line between ambient/"wallpaper" music and more melodic/narrative forms, and he explores that space very thoughtfully, with great results.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/The Art of Analog Diversion/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/The Art of Analog Diversion/cover_200.jpg', 1085036400, 14),
(1354, 548, 'Ambient Excursions', 'The aptly titled Ambient Excursions is Stargarden''s best-selling album to date, and provides the listener with a big, cosmic yet infinitely subtle listening experience: entire sonic geographies unfold slowly in front of Bobby DeVito''s wide lens, revealing far-away worlds of both pure sound and pure pattern laid overtop the sound, like shadown and light playing on a sonic topography. The trace doppler patterns in "Millenia" create perhaps one of the most subtly captivatingly ambient tracks of all time while the oh-so laid back groove of "Astral Dub" lends temporary structure to the cosmic clouds. Put it on and let it slowly unfurl.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/Ambient Excursions/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/Ambient Excursions/cover_200.jpg', 1085036400, 12),
(1355, 548, 'Five', '"Stargarden "FIVE" is quite possibly the ONLY ambient recording ever made during a hurricane. Bobby D was living in Key West playing music, and happened to be there during Hurricane Ernesto. The Keys were evacuated, but Bobby decided to stay and ride the storm out. He rented a bunch of gear, got some supplies and refreshments, and hunkered down to wait for the arriving storm. During the storm, Stargarden "FIVE" was composed and recorded, using 24bit/96K high resoution audio.\n\nWhile the album is quite a bit shorter than most of the other Stargarden releases, "FIVE" has a unique sound and feel. Perhaps the act of creating ambient music in the midst of a hurricane helped to make this album a bit different than the other Stargarden albums. This album is the quiet before the storm, as well as the sounds created in the eye of the hurricane.\n\nGive "FIVE" a listen...this may well be the most unique and beautiful album by Stargarden yet!"\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/Five/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/Five/cover_200.jpg', 1236412800, 14),
(1356, 548, 'Music for Modern Listening', 'Appealingly low-key, Music for Modern Listening is Stargarden''s most cosmpolitan and groove-oriented work to date. "Mural" gets things off to a nicely urbane start with it''s crushed beats and subtle, vespertine tones, and Bobby DeVito follows this up with "Perdido"--a great juxtaposition of blurry bells with delicately eccentric rhythms. We also really like the suave bass and artfully textured synths of "Termoli" as well as the sultry sounding "Rutile." This album may lean slightly away from the space music sound for which Stargarden is known, but it can be good to come back down to earth every now and then, and Music for Modern Listening proves just how great Stargarden sounds back on good ol'' terra firma. Cover photography by Michael Mattis.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/Music for Modern Listening/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/Music for Modern Listening/cover_200.jpg', 1108108800, 11),
(1357, 548, 'Step Off', 'When Stargarden tells you to "step off," it''s clear that they mean "off" of the normal platform of daily life, and directly into a big night sky of softly pulsating ambient sounds. Bobby Devito has a real talent for juxtaposing interesting sonic patterns, which then somehow become these ever-expanding and shrinking sound forms and give his music a natural, even organic quality. It''s ambient/space music, sure, but it''s very structured, so it lends itself well to studying and working. Tracks we particularly like include "From Here to There," "Anger Management," and "The Dream Is Alive."\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/Step Off/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stargarden/Step Off/cover_200.jpg', 1096959600, 14),
(1358, 549, 'Star Of The Sea', 'We simply never tire of the hauntingly elegant Star ot the Sea by Stellamara. Sonja Drakulich''s rich, siren-like vocals are paired with stately, reverential beats, and an exceptionally rare mood is established as a result-one both seductive and solemn. This album draws on the most arcane and ancient melodies from 13th and 15th century Galacia, Croatia, and Persia, and makes them relevant and engaging. "Zephyrus" is an incredible track, as is "Immrama"-an almost incomprehensibly subtle medieval Irish song concerning a hero''s sea journey to the Otherworld. "Stellamara cut directly to the devotional essence of music." -SF Weekly . Highly recommended.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stellamara/Star Of The Sea/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stellamara/Star Of The Sea/cover_200.jpg', 1071216000, 21),
(1359, 550, 'Goode Christemas Musicke', '"Goode" is definitely an understatement here. Recorded at St. Paul''s United Church with the Niagra Children''s Chorus and the Con Brio Choir, the freshly angelic choral pieces on this album album sparkle like sugar on a plum. Stephane Potvin is known for his energizing effect on the choral groups with which he works, and you can definitely hear his influence in the music. Pure delight.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stephane Potvin and the Con Brio Choir/Goode Christemas Musicke/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stephane Potvin and the Con Brio Choir/Goode Christemas Musicke/cover_200.jpg', 1196409600, 33),
(1360, 551, 'Set Future', 'Stephens'' first album Set Future was two years in production. The songs are original electronic ambient techno with a distinct sound and production technique for each of the twelve tracks.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stephen Schweyen/Set Future/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stephen Schweyen/Set Future/cover_200.jpg', 1246777200, 7),
(1361, 552, 'Are You Out There', 'With just 4 songs, this EP contains some of Sterling''s earliest work and his first foray into more rhythmic space oriented music. If you enjoy his other material, you are sure to want to check out some of these songs which set the stage for future full releases such as "Paradox" and "Spaceflight".\n\nEnjoy a glimpse into some more of Sterling''s impressive catalog. He is really pleased to be able to have the chance to share it with so many great listeners here on Magnatune and hopes you enjoy this and his other releases. "Are you out there?" marks Sterling''s eighth Magnatune release. (Album cover credit: NASA/JHU APL/CIW.)\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Are You Out There/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Are You Out There/cover_200.jpg', 1342767600, 10),
(1362, 552, 'Beyond Human', 'Similar to Sterling''s recent "Spaceflight", more space oriented music is to be had in this 7 song EP. If you enjoyed "Spaceflight" or any of Sterling''s more rhythmic ambient works, we are sure that you will also enjoy this new collection of songs as well. The journey continues. (Album cover credit: Cassini Imaging Team, ISS, JPL, ESA, NASA.)\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Beyond Human/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Beyond Human/cover_200.jpg', 1321776000, 10),
(1363, 552, 'Near You Always', 'In "Near You Always", Sterling contributes yet another stunning collection of heartfelt electronica to the Magnatune catalog. From the classical sounding "Orchestral" to the pure ambiance of "Love", Sterling continues to explore the realm of intimate electronic music in his own special way. These songs have a purity characteristic of Sterling''s earlier works, where emotion and beauty is the primary expression... songs that are simply straight from the heart. (Album cover credit: STS-41B, NASA.)\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Near You Always/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Near You Always/cover_200.jpg', 1298620800, 8),
(1364, 552, 'Paradox', 'Paradox is yet another creative electronic offering from the talented and very prolific artist "Sterling". Predominantly upbeat, this album covers lots of different ground. Produced after Sterling''s debut album (Sterling) yet never officially released until now, Magnatune is proud to allow listeners to appreciate another one of Sterling''s early works. Enjoy. (Album cover credit: Cassini Imaging Team, ISS, JPL, ESA, NASA.)\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Paradox/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Paradox/cover_200.jpg', 1310540400, 11),
(1365, 552, 'Spaceflight', 'With electronic music as intriguing as it is mysterious, Sterling''s "Spaceflight" is ready to transport listeners to another dimension of thought and sound. Arranged for the first time, some of Sterling''s most exotic space oriented works are now available in the form of this special new release. Put on a nice pair of headphones, turn off the lights, and be prepared to go on a journey into the unknown. (Album cover credit: NASA/Goddard/SDO AIA Team).\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Spaceflight/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Spaceflight/cover_200.jpg', 1320562800, 7),
(1366, 552, 'Sterling', 'Integrating trance, ambient, and breakbeat; Sterling has created a progressive sound that is uniquely his own. His style is represented by intensely emotive work dealing with themes such as loneliness, faith, and transcendence. In Sterling''s mind, it goes beyond music. He is struggling to create the soundtrack to a new reality, teeming with so much hope and possibility.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Sterling/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Sterling/cover_200.jpg', 1273302000, 8),
(1367, 552, 'Vega', 'Through a seamless integration of trance, ambience, and breakbeats; Sterling has created a progressive style that is uniquely his own. Marked by some of his most haunting, intimate and ambient earlier works, this release is destined to be another classic release by Sterling. Prepare to enter into the cosmic sublime with "Vega". (Album cover image from NASA: STS-41B)\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Vega/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Vega/cover_200.jpg', 1293177600, 8),
(1368, 552, 'Brave New World', 'On this second release, Sterling attempts a more thematic work dealing with the idea of the destruction and rebirth of the Earth. The various songs were produced and arranged within this conceptual framework. In the end, this album came to be a unique experience including more energetic songs along with the trademark ambient works that Sterling has come to be known for.\n\nIt makes for a unique and engaging listening experience that leaves it up to the listener as to what they want to feel and how they want to interpret things. Sterling loves to maintain a sense of mystique within his works and this album does just that.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Brave New World/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sterling/Brave New World/cover_200.jpg', 1279350000, 10),
(1369, 553, 'Hark, the Glad Sound!', 'Hark, the Glad Sound!: Celebrates the longing and yearning of the season of Advent with the Sweet Song of Dulcimers.\n\nI have longed and yearned to share the beauty of these tunes with you since writing Dulcimer-Friendly Worship in 1993. In the waiting, the arrangements grew and matured. May your soul be inspired to rejoice as you wait.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steve Eulberg/Hark, the Glad Sound!/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steve Eulberg/Hark, the Glad Sound!/cover_200.jpg', 1336460400, 29),
(1370, 553, 'I Celebrate Life!', 'Steve Eulberg weaves old-timey with blues and contemporary tunes adding original songs into a tapestry of "smile-inducing, toe-tapping folkgrass" that captivates and energizes audiences of all ages. In addition to the treasures of the past, Steve loves to create new traditions with the tunes that flow from the sweet river. Floating along with his dulcimers, his music is enjoyed throughout the US, Europe, the UK, New Zealand and Australia.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steve Eulberg/I Celebrate Life!/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steve Eulberg/I Celebrate Life!/cover_200.jpg', 1329206400, 15),
(1371, 553, 'Soaring', 'This recording has long been requested by my audiences, who wanted to capture the live music experience at home. It is most like my live shows where I trade instruments and weave together original and traditional songs with instrumental tunes as I share the "sweet music" of dulcimers.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steve Eulberg/Soaring/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steve Eulberg/Soaring/cover_200.jpg', 1340002800, 14),
(1372, 553, '''Twas in the Moon of Wintertime', '''Twas in the Moon of Wintertime\n\n"I''ve always loved these minor mode tunes and they are especially suited to dulcimers. May they breathe peace and hope into your soul"\n- Steve Eulberg\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steve Eulberg/''Twas in the Moon of Wintertime/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steve Eulberg/''Twas in the Moon of Wintertime/cover_200.jpg', 1355558400, 26),
(1373, 554, 'Portrait of an English Harpsichord', 'Steven Devine plays a wonderful selection of pieces on a 1756 Kirckman Harpsichord, each specifically chosen to highlight the tonal characteristics of this amazing instrument. Devine is a wonderfully lively player, and we really appreciate that he chose music by composers who were appreciated in their lifetime, but who are now largely neglected. A deeply charming album.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steven Devine/Portrait of an English Harpsichord/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Steven Devine/Portrait of an English Harpsichord/cover_200.jpg', 1179212400, 11),
(1374, 555, 'Mozart Klavier Trios', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Streicher Trio/Mozart Klavier Trios/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Streicher Trio/Mozart Klavier Trios/cover_200.jpg', 1217055600, 24),
(1375, 555, 'Musica da Camera', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Streicher Trio/Musica da Camera/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Streicher Trio/Musica da Camera/cover_200.jpg', 1217055600, 14),
(1376, 555, 'Two Romantic Piano Trios by Women Composers', '', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Streicher Trio/Two Romantic Piano Trios by Women Composers/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Streicher Trio/Two Romantic Piano Trios by Women Composers/cover_200.jpg', 1217055600, 15),
(1377, 556, 'Dirnix', 'Strojovna 7''s best selling Magnatune album, Dirnix brings in a nice kind of subtlety and restraint to the often overly-hyper genre of techno. It''s still driving, mind you, but in a thoughtful, light-touch kind of way, and we also appreciate how simple and straight forward this album is. "Dezert" is a particular standout. Great workout music.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/Dirnix/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/Dirnix/cover_200.jpg', 1054969200, 7),
(1378, 556, 'III', 'From the upbeat near-disco of the opener "Zabudni na to," to the atmospherically cosmopolitan "Loopatchka," III by Strojovna 7 really shows how they''ve grown from a more experimental techno act from one that knows how to structure tracks exceptionally well. More rhythmically savvy and detailed than any of their work to date, IIIhas some great tracks on it and is absolutely perfect workout music. Our favorite Strojovna 7 album.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/III/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/III/cover_200.jpg', 1082358000, 8),
(1379, 556, 'Mirnix', 'The follow-up to Strojovna 7''s Dirnix, Mirnix is also a simple and straight forward album of their light-touch techno, but this one has a slightly more textured and cosmopolitan feel to it, which we like. There''s a nice selection of styles represented here, from the subtly boppy and urbane "Lighting Fly" to the aerodynamically lush "TEXEMPO."\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/Mirnix/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/Mirnix/cover_200.jpg', 1054969200, 5),
(1380, 556, 'Number 1', 'Strojovna 7''s first Magnatune release Number 1 is a an experimental yet groovy techno album, with some great sonic layering and treats in it. "Ambi Crack" is our favorite of these with its pebbly textures, but there are other standouts too including "Bycygel" and the lushly ambient closer "Ambicious." [sic] A straight forward techno album with a light touch.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/Number 1/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/Number 1/cover_200.jpg', 1054969200, 7),
(1381, 556, 'Switch On - Switch Off', 'This album''s highly sapient tech-house features Strojovna 07''s perfectly proportioned beats and highly textured auditory treats. Unapologetically repetitive and driving, this is the sort of techno that switches off your monkey mind and switches on the dancing feet. Tracks like "Logistic" and "Lienkey" show off Strojovna 07''s ability to bring in softer, bionic-feeling sounds that create a nicely dimensional, futuristic tone.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/Switch On - Switch Off/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Strojovna 07/Switch On - Switch Off/cover_200.jpg', 1148886000, 10),
(1382, 557, '16-9', 'A one man analogue orchestra producing a soundtrack that needs no film.\n\nWhat''s that? It''s the noise of 16 tireless months in the studio. It''s like listening to three decades in the music industry.\n\nIt''s also the sound of homemade, hand-welded percussion instruments, without an audible drum. Impressive then, that it''s one man and his Mac in Northamptonshire, with two great ears.\n\nHis debut long-player, 16:9, is a soundtrack to the greatest of epics - seamless seconds of breathtaking aural vision. Fleeting sonic supernovas that inspire emotion and command attention, while blending beautifully into the natural world of the listener. Think the exploratory moments of Vangelis'' Bladerunner score, here rooted in the instrumentalism so vital to the early 21st Century, and its broad definition of ''popular music''.\n\nBird songs can be heard, punctuating an almost visible landscape, such is the tangible depth to these compositions. Pianos fall gently into background space, as strings, synths and a host of other, often unexpected musical tools combine to create a tragic, heroic and captivating movie in the mind. A road trip on a mass scale, the sombre closing credits of Cherry Blossom Falls offer up Vienna in violin arrangements, while Kaiyu-Shiki transports you to the eponymous Japanese garden, via a thousand crystalised chimes.\n\nDarker moments abound in Memories Lost, as decomposing tape creates a feeling of faded photography and silent, battle-scorched earth. Meanwhile, The Fire Within recounts a town springing into life on a dark winter''s morning, as ambient noise becomes hook and then melody. It might seem lazy to reel off disconnected, imagined instances. But then few films have been this vivid in the landscapes and atmospheres they create.\n\nStuart Sweeney has spent countless hours exploring the English countryside surrounding his home and the sounds to be found, and more importantly recorded, therein. But, far from abstract, all 12 tracks offer something reminiscent of Yann Tiersen''s cinematic power, blended with the electronic-experimentation of The Heritage Orchestra. And there''s something wholly new too. Far from field music, this is a sublime one-man symphony.\n\nAnd that''s just what makes this release so inspired. Part contemporary classical, part sound art, all compelling. There''s no tunes here, just stories being told, moments getting captured and emotions laid bare. All the while remaining under the control of a technically disciplined musical aficionado. Hear the tracklist individually, or hear it all as one. Either way, from beginning to end, while under Sweeney''s spell, you won''t be doing much else.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stuart Sweeney/16-9/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Stuart Sweeney/16-9/cover_200.jpg', 1295942400, 10),
(1383, 558, 'Designer Bliss', 'Wow. Suchita Parte has an incredibly beautiful voice, and Designer Bliss really brings out the natural softness and femininity of it. The opening track, "Tvamekam Sharannyam" is so deeply pretty, as is "Pandurangashtakam" and "Narayanam,"" but really the entire album is a very successful, subtle pop update of a classical Indian style. Parte''s dulcet and delicate voice is at the exact center of things (where it should be), but she never pushes it or tries to dominate the overall sound. Completely sublime, highly recommended.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suchita Parte/Designer Bliss/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suchita Parte/Designer Bliss/cover_200.jpg', 1125903600, 14),
(1384, 559, 'Just You and Me', 'An album dedicated purely to the acoustically rich and rewarding sound of the veena, a stringed instrument of ancient South Indian history and lore.\n\nFeaturing a range of ragas, starting from morning prayerful melodies of Bowli, the straight forward Gowla, on to the unique and awe inspiring dignity of the raga Todi, winding on to the flavor of an Indian afternoon in the monsoon with a rhythmic free flowing exposition of the raga Kirvani, and then over to the beauty and charm of the alluring Karaharapriya, on to the laidback Purvikalyani and a lullaby to end it all.\n\nSome of these are interpretations of classical gems, others are extempore free flowing explorations into the form and characteristics of the raga. A traditional musical offering of pure South Indian Carnatic classical music, from the artist and the instrument, to God.\n\nImagine Indian temples, religious festivals, spiritual quests, contemplation, introspection and the soul''s awakening.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suchitra Lata/Just You and Me/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suchitra Lata/Just You and Me/cover_200.jpg', 1406358000, 94),
(1385, 559, 'Lava Lounge', 'Stylishly contemplative with a twist of red hot active lava on top! That''s the sound of this album. I wanted to reflect on the two constant movements within oneself, one inwards and the other outwards. I have attempted to capture the spirit of those subtle movements in this album.\n\nWith Coral Symphony, Sunbrella, Blue Petal Lake and Turquoise Beat, it''s the introspective, looking within movement, then on towards an outward swing with Star Filter, French Lounge, Purplesse Oblige, Lava Lounge, Riviera Magenta and ending with a very socially interactive Cocktail Disco. Musical portrait of the self!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suchitra Lata/Lava Lounge/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suchitra Lata/Lava Lounge/cover_200.jpg', 1414825200, 79),
(1386, 559, 'Trip Along', 'Sophisticated electronica with sultry vocals; female, male and a choir, eclectic in its choice of languages, from English to French via Latin connected with ancient Indian poems (known as cheez in Hindustani classical music) and the hoary Dravidian tongue of Tamil, electronically filtered samples; this album explores musical textures from every angle and language!\n\nThe ambiance also shifts in a pleasant kaleidoscopic array, from a happy monsoon welcoming song, to seductive love songs sung to a lover, some also to God, to metaphysical and linguistic contemplation on silence and words, to the grand Latin phrase "Veritas Vos Liberabit"!\n\nStep into a luxurious resort tucked away under the green fronds in a magical forest, or far out on in deep space, high above the clouds and the mist of a moonlit night, or simply deep inside the recesses of your heart and find yourself wafting peacefully into your zen lounge where you are always welcome! So, trip along!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suchitra Lata/Trip Along/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suchitra Lata/Trip Along/cover_200.jpg', 1420704000, 56),
(1387, 560, 'Chameleon', '"The hidden secret in this music is balm to the soul" writes one of many UK therapists who use the album as an aid to relaxation and meditation. Some of the music on the album was used in a pilot study at Bristol Cancer Help Centre, UK, and results showed listeners experiencing an increased sense of well-being and relaxation and a significant drop in tension. A physiological test also showed significantly increased levels of salivary IgA (which is an indicator of immune status) and measured decreased levels of cortisol (a stress indicator), marking relaxation and improved mood.\n\nComments from patients included the following:\n"The power of the music uplifted the spirit"\n"I felt totally at peace"\n"Music to feed the soul and fortify the body"\n"The power of the music to disarm and enter people, to lift up their spirits and make them whole is amazing."\n\nTry it!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sulis/Chameleon/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sulis/Chameleon/cover_200.jpg', 1264147200, 12),
(1388, 560, 'The Vision Of Enoch', 'This is an extraordinary meditative piece, and was recorded in Douai Abbey in the UK. This piece is a setting of inspirational words translated from the Gospel of the Essenes by Edmond Bordeaux Szekely and is gloriously sung by the acclaimed counter-tenor Robin Blaze and soprano Jackie Evill, accompanied only by three pitched singing bowls.\n\nThe words are reproduced by kind permission of the International Biogenic Society, Nelson, British Columbia, Canada and are available when you download the artwork.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sulis/The Vision Of Enoch/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sulis/The Vision Of Enoch/cover_200.jpg', 1271401200, 10),
(1389, 560, 'Sitting on the windowsill of Heaven - Celia Harper', 'Sulis encourages the listener to be embraced by the sounds of its unusual instruments and haunting melodies, creating a musical space for personal reflection. Tuneful and touching music with influences of early baroque and Celtic.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sulis/Sitting on the windowsill of Heaven - Celia Harper/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sulis/Sitting on the windowsill of Heaven - Celia Harper/cover_200.jpg', 1246777200, 13),
(1390, 561, 'Into Heaven', 'After releasing Sun Palace''s latest album in December 2005, we were lucky enough to get our hands on this band''s year 2000 debut CD?Into Heaven?which received four out of five stars from AllMusic.com and shows the more wistful, contemplative side of vocalist Andriette Redmann''s talents. Written and recorded after a series of personal tragedies in her life, Into Heaven''s palpable sense of melancholy serves as the ideal counterpoint to Redmann''s lighter-than-air voice, and we suspect that fans of Sarah McLachlan and The Sundays will love this album.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sun Palace/Into Heaven/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sun Palace/Into Heaven/cover_200.jpg', 1138694400, 7),
(1391, 561, 'Give Me a Perfect World', 'Angel-voiced Andriette Redmann teams up with Smithereens frontman John Rokosny to make a luxuriant yet lighter than air pop-rock creation. Give Me A Perfect World does more than live up to the promise of the first Sun Palace album, Into Heaven?it is, in fact, an even stronger and more impressive demonstration of Redmann''s talents. Highlights include the idyllic title track, the courtly and fateful "Round and Round," as well as the deeply dreamy "Familiar Voices." This album features legendary drummer Jerry Marotta (Peter Gabriel/Paul McCartney/Elvis Costello) and cellist Jane Scarpantoni (Springsteen/Lou Reed/REM). Highly expressive, highly listenable.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sun Palace/Give Me a Perfect World/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sun Palace/Give Me a Perfect World/cover_200.jpg', 1132905600, 12),
(1392, 562, 'Sundowner', 'Straight out of Manchester, a startlingly fantastic first album from Sundowner who have an expertly trippy, drone heavy sound. Relentless walls of sonic heaviness somehow transmogrify into ethereal bliss with vocals that just absolutely _fly_. on tracks like "High on Strife" and "The Morning Won''t Wait." One of the most superlative rock offerings we''ve ever made on Magnatune. Check it out.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sundowner/Sundowner/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sundowner/Sundowner/cover_200.jpg', 1198137600, 6),
(1393, 563, 'The Afro Dub EP', 'The Afro-Dub EP collects the band''s first two 45 inch vinyl releases on Electric Cowbell Records, plus some bonus tracks and remixes. Released in 2012, the "Single Payer" single features the band''s original plus a three-part re-imagining of the track by dub and ska legend Victor Rice. "We Will Begin Again," also released in 2012, adds a dub version by Brooklyn dub engineer Prince Polo and a hallucinogenic remix by DJ Trainwreck (bassist Ezra Gale''s alter ego). Finally, a dub version of the unreleased "March of the Frogs" by Oakland, CA producer Myles Boisin rounds out the EP.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Super Hi-Fi/The Afro Dub EP/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Super Hi-Fi/The Afro Dub EP/cover_200.jpg', 1365922800, 7),
(1394, 564, 'Algoriddims', 'This album is a sound explosion! Inspired by classical harmonies and beats that groove to hell, Superdirt2 (Ras Tilo (electro, programming) and Kapt''n Dirt (cello) recorded their first full-length album.\n\nThe album is a synergism of what they believe music ought to be: nine tracks consumed by a deep emotional feeling, completed by an ecstatic and overwhelming groove character. All of this is conveyed through their compulsive need to share their passion of music to everyone who is ready to celebrate the party, which is called life.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Superdirt2/Algoriddims/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Superdirt2/Algoriddims/cover_200.jpg', 1389254400, 44),
(1395, 565, 'Among the Lasses, songs of Robert Burns (1759-1796)', 'Soprano Susan Rode Morris and harpsichordist Phebe Craig offer a look at one way Burns heard his songs played. Among the Lasses is the second in the trio collection of the beloved songs of Scotland''s national poet Robert Burns.\n\nThey have gone back to the original tunes and have created their own bass lines and arrangements based on their knowledge of 18th century music. The poetry and tunes are delightful and refreshing. Burns was not a composer, but a great collector of his national music. He took existing songs that were only partly remembered and rewrote the verses to "mend them" as he wrote. He set his poetry to his favorite fiddle or bagpipe tunes.\n\nReviews include American Record Guide''s comments: "Morris sings with wonderful abandon, never losing control of tone or intonation. She has a lovely lilting voice, sometimes changing her tome quality to a surprising degree as she finds the right timbre for a special moment." "Morris and Craig approach the world of 18th century music making in a most profound way."\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Susan Rode Morris/Among the Lasses, songs of Robert Burns (1759-1796)/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Susan Rode Morris/Among the Lasses, songs of Robert Burns (1759-1796)/cover_200.jpg', 1348988400, 14),
(1396, 565, 'Between Late and Early - Romantic Songs of Robert Burns (1759-1796)', 'When singer Susan Rode Morris and harpsichordist Phebe Craig met and performed with renowned Scottish fiddle Alasdair Fraser, they discovered the songs of Robert Burns, Scotland''s National Poet.\n\nFew people know that Burns collected his native tunes and songs and created poetry to many of the wonderful fiddle tunes he loved.\n\nFeatured on National Public Radio on Robert Burns birthday, this album includes the original version of Auld Lang Syne, which has been played on public radio on New Year''s Eve. "This wonderful performance engendered more response that just about any recording we can remember, in this or any other year" said Martin Goldsmith of Performance Today of NPR. "Hauntingly beautiful renditions, skilled, loving, exquisite."\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Susan Rode Morris/Between Late and Early - Romantic Songs of Robert Burns (1759-1796)/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Susan Rode Morris/Between Late and Early - Romantic Songs of Robert Burns (1759-1796)/cover_200.jpg', 1346742000, 14),
(1397, 565, 'Ohhh Henry! - Songs of Henry Purcell', 'Henry Purcell was one of England''s greatest song writers of the 17th century. This recording is a veritable potpourri for the listener with two dozen songs, some rarely recorded, offering a wide range of expression.\n\nReviews have praised this recording for its beauty:\n\n"Seldom have these songs of Purcell been given such thoughtful renditions by both singer and harpsichordist...a gorgeous voice, extraordinary continuo playing." American Record Guide.\n\n"If you love beautiful soprano voices, communicative singing or great Purcell songs, I recommend this disc." Fanfare Magazine.\n\n"Ohhh Henry! joyfully transcends... Of the three new soprano collections Morris has the richest imagination in both the selection and interpretation of the music." Washington Post\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Susan Rode Morris/Ohhh Henry! - Songs of Henry Purcell/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Susan Rode Morris/Ohhh Henry! - Songs of Henry Purcell/cover_200.jpg', 1352012400, 14),
(1398, 566, 'Enchanted Wind', 'We think Enchanted Wind is global flutist Suzanne Teng''s best yet: seven long tracks that showcase her sinuous, slowly unfolding melodies on the alto flute, bass flute, and the rare contrabass flute. The mood here is profoundly quiet and serene; there are no beats at all and the accompaniment on harp, dulcimer, harmonium, and zither is delicate and minimal. The approach shows the full scope of Teng''s talent too; stripped of adornment, you really hear how masterful her tone and breath control are, and the music itself is timeless. Listen after a stressful day and marvel, as we did, at how completely it dissolves tension.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suzanne Teng/Enchanted Wind/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suzanne Teng/Enchanted Wind/cover_200.jpg', 1154329200, 92),
(1399, 566, 'Miles Beyond', 'The Chinese define "chi" as the circulating life energy inherent in all things, and on Miles Beyond, best-selling Magnatune artist Suzanne Teng''s music is absolutely suffused with it. Serene and mysterious, yet also possessing a subtly lively, spirited quality, this album features her gorgeous contemporary instrumental pieces-soundscapes with exquisitely close wooden flute harmonies, fantastic tribal percussion, thoughtful strings and an overall feeling of well being. "A meticulously composed and arranged work that uses traditional sounds for a new pan-global music." -John Diliberto, Public Radio International\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suzanne Teng/Miles Beyond/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suzanne Teng/Miles Beyond/cover_200.jpg', 1125903600, 37),
(1400, 566, 'Mystic Journey', 'A lithe, fluid, and graceful album of Suzanne Teng''s exquisite pan-global instrumental music, Mystic Journey really delivers the goods with its fantastic performance by Suzanne Teng and her band, as well as its great production values. Teng plays with masterful ease, and it''s easy to detect her joy in the performance of these beautiful east-meets-west compositions. Al Martinez of the L.A. Times says: "Suzanne Teng plays music for the soul. This music will stay with me for a lifetime."\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suzanne Teng/Mystic Journey/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Suzanne Teng/Mystic Journey/cover_200.jpg', 1125903600, 52),
(1401, 567, 'Svanevit', 'John writes: Magnatune musician Tim Rayborn introduced me to his Swedish Folk musician friend Erik Ask-Upmark. They''ve played together many times (most recently when Erik was on tour, and passed through San Francisco). Erik has many projects, all focussed on traditional Swedish folk music, so be sure to check out his solo album, his album with Anna Rynefors entitled "Dram" and his group Svanevit.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Svanevit/Svanevit/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Svanevit/Svanevit/cover_200.jpg', 1234080000, 26),
(1402, 568, 'New World', 'Svara is an acoustic poly-musical ensemble: we each bring musical ideas and experience from all over the world: India, Bali, Brazil, Europe (Eastern and Western), America. Rock, jazz, classical, contemporary creative music.\n\nOur goal is an innovative foray into unexplored musical spaces. We are 4 musicians who have experienced deep training in a variety of traditional music forms (including western traditions) from renowned and insightful teachers. We have tried to channel that training, that exposure into a unique and organic musical expression. All of our music incorporates improvisation - some pieces are almost completely improvised; others have an open section for spontaneous exploration.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Svara/New World/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Svara/New World/cover_200.jpg', 1315378800, 14),
(1403, 569, 'Sweet Teen Killing Machine', 'Self-described as "horror-rock," Portland, Oregon-based, hard-hitting, heavy rock act Sweet Teen Killing Machine deliver an exhilarating, self-titled full-length. Comprised of ten tracks, the album delivers melodic, yet hard rock, each song the soundtrack to a fictitious horror movie.\n\nSweet Teen Killing Machine make guitar-rock that is as frightening as it is heart pounding. From the supernatural horror of album opener, "Curse of the Bloodwolf" and the album''s first single, "City of the Shrieking Dead." To the more human terror of "Virgin White" and "New Meat," Sweet Teen Killing Machine can only be described as the sound of an insane rock beast that always seem to be chasing you.\n\nIdeally, Sweet Teen Killing Machine wants to create soundtracks to movies; so, while writing soundtracks to fictitious movies, they take a scenario, a scene from a movie they''ve made up in their heads, and create the ideal soundtrack for it.\n\nOriginally a basement project between friends, jamming and having a good time, Sweet Teen Killing Machine''s debut grew out of that. Never meant to release a record, the ten tracks that compile the album came out sounding so powerful; the members of the band couldn''t help but release it.\n\nThose that hear their debut full-length will take notice and go, "more please." Again, like the victims and villains in the horror movies they write soundtracks for.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sweet Teen Killing Machine/Sweet Teen Killing Machine/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Sweet Teen Killing Machine/Sweet Teen Killing Machine/cover_200.jpg', 1305702000, 4),
(1404, 570, 'Deep Life', 'Magnatune artist William Brooks aka "Swivel Neck Jones" makes a foray into atmospheric electronica and suceeds admirably at creating a mellow, underwater-ish vibe. Hammond and vibraphone swirl around crisp, mid-tempo beats for a great listen-while-you-work album. Viva the life aqautic!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Swivel Neck Jones/Deep Life/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Swivel Neck Jones/Deep Life/cover_200.jpg', 1193814000, 4),
(1405, 571, 'Mix A Small Impact', 'One of the salient features of electronic music is the ability to communicate without the use of standardized elements like choruses, vocals, or often even melodies. In "Mix A Small Impact" SynchDub communicate with sounds that''s not only full of simple emotions but pries out of the soul, the essence of artistry. This album is a diverse work and mix of driving electronic basslines and beats, but also full with nostalgic and melancholic sounds.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SynchDub/Mix A Small Impact/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/SynchDub/Mix A Small Impact/cover_200.jpg', 1305961200, 4),
(1406, 572, 'JS Bach Laboratory', 'J. S. Bach Laboratory / the flute ensemble Spark\n\nThis all-Bach album features original arrangements that seek to capture the spirit of Bach''s original ideas. It was recorded in Hakodate City Art Hall in 27 November 2000 and was first released in 2000 to commemorate the 250th anniversary of Bach''s death.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/JS Bach Laboratory/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/JS Bach Laboratory/cover_200.jpg', 1329724800, 23),
(1407, 572, 'JS Bach Flute Sonatas and Partita', 'J.S. Bach''s Flute Sonatas and Partita were chosen for this album as they were particularly well suited to capturing the sound and atmosphere of the hall in its pre-renovation state. Sonate h-moll fur Flote und obligates Cembalo BWV 1030 is played here with flute and cembalo without basso continuo. Partita a-moll fur Flote allein BWV 1013, known in the only surviving 18th-century manuscript as ''Solo pour une flote traversiere par J. S. Bach'' is for unaccompanied flute. Sonate e-moll fur Flote und Basso Continuo BWV 1034 and Sonate E-dur fur Flote und Basso Continuo BWV 1035 are played with cembalo and basso continuo, and in this album, the basso continuo part is realized with a viola da gamba.\n\nThe recording is carefully designed to record not only the sound of the instruments but the ambient sound of the old hall using a highly sensitive one-point stereo microphone system, a specially-tuned tube amp, and a high-precision clock system. The high standard mode (96 kHz, 24 bit) and the folding of full information into CD format enable the listener to appreciate the hi-fidelity sound and precise nuances in performance.\n\nTakashi Matsuishi plays J. S. Bach Flute Sonatas and Partita, recorded at the Hakodate City Community Centre 2011. This album provides a record of Hakodate City Community Centre Hall''s unique acoustic qualities. The hall was built in 1933 in the old part of the city and used not only for various lectures and meetings but also as a concert hall. In the later part of the twentieth century, it fell into disrepair, neglect and was sadly used less and less frequently until it was eventually forgotten. Flautist Takashi Matsuishi rediscovered the hall, noting its qualities as worthy of renovation and, since 2007, he has revived its usage by producing a matinee concert series and promoting its unique properties. The hall''s renewed popularity with audiences and the media interest in its revival led to a decision by Hakodate City Council to pursue its renovation, bringing its facilities up to modern standards.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/JS Bach Flute Sonatas and Partita/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/JS Bach Flute Sonatas and Partita/cover_200.jpg', 1342767600, 18),
(1408, 572, 'Selected Duets', 'The music for this album was chosen from the world-famous educational music book for flute beginners: Selected Duets for Flute: Volume II, compiled and edited by H. Voxman in Rubank Educational library. The music, performed by Spark''s Yutaka Okawachi and Takashi Matsuishi, was recorded by recording engineer Yasuyuki Nitta in Tazawa-ko Town Hall in Akita Prefecture, Japan on 4-6 August, 2004.\n\nThe album is a useful resource for flute beginners and is ideal as background music in a variety of contexts.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/Selected Duets/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/Selected Duets/cover_200.jpg', 1338447600, 15),
(1409, 572, 'Omogin', 'The title of this album Omogin means ''memory is like a silver flute'' in Japanese. The album contains a range of attractive ensemble pieces for two to five flutes.\n\nIt includes J. B. Boismortier''s Concerts pour 5 flute-traversieres sans basse Op15 (important repertoire pieces for flute ensembles on the competition circuit), as well as Spark''s original arrangements of J. S. Bach''s Toccata und Fuge BWV 565.\n\nThe music was recorded in Hakodate City Art Hall on 18 August 2001 and in Toyama Art Creation Centre on 14 - 17 August 2002.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/Omogin/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/Omogin/cover_200.jpg', 1335596400, 14),
(1410, 572, 'Preludes', 'Preludes / the flute ensemble Spark: this album features a wide variety of music from J. S. Bach to Ravel. All of the music is arranged by Spark and was recorded live in Hakodate City Art Hall on 22 March 1999. Photographer Hiroji Fukuda received the Grand Prix of the Fuji Film Commercial Photographer Competition in 1999 with the photo of Spark that features on the cover of this album. This album, originally released in 1999, is Spark''s first album.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/Preludes/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Takashi Matsuishi Spark/Preludes/cover_200.jpg', 1327132800, 22),
(1411, 573, 'Beethoven Piano and Cello Works', 'One of the most beautiful pairings of piano and cello ever written, Beethoven Piano and Cello Works features the seamless musical partnership of Tanya Tomkins and Eric Zivian. Together, they handle the precarious balance between these two instruments beautifully, and even leverage it in order to bring out the intelligent passion and elegant drama that defines Beethoven''s music. A wonderful album.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Tanya Tomkins and Eric Zivian/Beethoven Piano and Cello Works/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Tanya Tomkins and Eric Zivian/Beethoven Piano and Cello Works/cover_200.jpg', 1071302400, 43),
(1412, 574, 'Telemann Trio Berlin', 'The largest newspaper in Germany recently described Telemann Trio Berlin''s work as "the delicate correspondence of three musicians," and this collection of selected works by Vivaldi, Telemann, and C.P. Bach perfectly captures the outstanding ensemble playing of these three accomplished artists. Intimate in character even for chamber music, this album is a lovely assortment of quietly graceful selections, from genteel to joyous.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Telemann Trio Berlin/Telemann Trio Berlin/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Telemann Trio Berlin/Telemann Trio Berlin/cover_200.jpg', 1153378800, 23);
INSERT INTO `albums` (`id`, `artist_id`, `name`, `description`, `cover`, `cover_small`, `release_date`, `popularity`) VALUES
(1413, 575, 'Teslim', 'Teslim (Tes-LEEM) is a Turkish word that means both "commit" and "surrender," It is also a musical term for a "recurring musical theme" in Turkish music. Like a character who appears in a sequel or a repeating line in a poem, the teslim invokes a feeling of returning home. For Flexer and Hegedus, creating this music has given them a deep sense of coming home both personally and musically.\n\nThese two composers have been profoundly moved and inspired by the power of traditional Greek, Turkish, Arabic, Jewish and Balkan musics. Flexer and Hegedus listen to, study and perform repertoire from these traditions with reverence and respect. In their work as composers, they are searching for their own voice - one that takes inspiration from tradition and follows where it leads.\n\nThey feel fortunate to have found in one another a kindred spirit who shares a common artistic vision. For them, the recurring theme, the teslim, is that the trust they have in one another musically has allowed them to both commit and surrender to their own individual voices, hopefully creating something larger than either one of them. It is their wish that this voice reaches into you and that you are inspired to continue the cycle of beauty and hope.\n\nMusicians featured on Teslim:\nKaila Flexer - violin, viola\nGari Hegedus - lauoto, baglama, bulgari, divan, cura, lavta, oud, tarhu, frame drum, penny whistle\nShira Kammen and Julian Smedley - fiddles\nOlov Johansson - nyckelharpa\nLiza Wallace - harp\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Teslim/Teslim/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/Teslim/Teslim/cover_200.jpg', 1414825200, 97),
(1414, 576, 'The Furious Guitar', 'Think..."What if Steve Vai, Metallica, King Crimson, Ozzy, Yes, Sabbath, Nirvana, The Beatles, STP, NIN, Zeppelin, Van Halen and Frank Zappa were all thrown in a blender with some Tequila and strawberries - that just BEGINS to tell the story...\n\nThe Furious Guitar was recorded for one reason only... To rearrange the status quo landscape of the last decade of rock and roll. "I am fucking bored to fucking tears by today''s bands," is a mike e.t. quote.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Alien Mike ET/The Furious Guitar/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Alien Mike ET/The Furious Guitar/cover_200.jpg', 1268380800, 6),
(1415, 576, 'Psychobabble', 'What if you took Steve Vai, King Crimson, Frank Zappa, old Genesis, Mahavishnu Orchestra, Nirvana, Black Sabbath and Kelly Clarkson, rolled them into a joint, smoked them and then did a shot of Patron??? You''d KINDA get the Alien, but then again, nobody gets the Alien, he''s too f***ed up... Uranus is a crazy place...\n\nGuitar madness and excess of the highest order. A psychedelic yet hard rocking album of gigantic complexity. Just listen to the silky, scintillating, seductive, suicidal, sonorous, sweaty strains of music and let your brain cells melt...\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Alien Mike ET/Psychobabble/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Alien Mike ET/Psychobabble/cover_200.jpg', 1305961200, 6),
(1416, 577, 'Bach arranging and arranged', 'Bach arranging and arranged : What happens when great composers arrange each other''s works? J. S. Bach gave Pergolesi''s Stabat Mater a new text and a new viola part, making a fresh piece that speaks both of Germany and Italy. Mozart gave string players the pleasure of playing fugues from the Well-tempered Clavier II: fresh arrangements by The Bach Players complete the set of all the four-part fugues from this work.\n\nJ. S. Bach, Fugues from the Well-tempered Clavier II, arranged for string quartet by W.A. Mozart, K 405, c. 1782 G. B. Pergolesi, Stabat Mater in F minor, arranged by J. S. Bach on the text of Psalm 51: ''Tilge, Hochster, meine Sunden'', BWV 1083, c. 1746\n\nRachel Elliott: soprano\nSally Bruce-Payne: alto\nNicolette Moonen: violin\nRodolfo Richter: violin\nHuw Daniel: violin\nSophie Barber: violin\nAnne Schumann: viola\nAlison McGillivray: cello\nElizabeth Bradley: double bass\nPawel Siwczak: organ\n\n"The performance is a delight from start to finish. All too often this music is spoilt by the uneven results and almost invariable rivalry stemming from the partnership of a soprano with a counter-tenor. Neither Rachel Elliott nor Sally Bruce-Payne is over-assertive or over-eager to take centre stage, much preferring to preserve the penitential context of the piece and the ethereal beauty of the music ... In short, no rival version that I have heard on disc, and there are several, matches this one. Well-judged tempos and an ideal recorded sound set the seal on a particularly satisfying issue. To hell with the credit crunch, go and buy it, at once."\nNicholas Anderson, International Record Review, February 2009\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bach Players/Bach arranging and arranged/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bach Players/Bach arranging and arranged/cover_200.jpg', 1316242800, 32),
(1417, 577, 'Every One a Chaconne', 'Every one a chaconne\n\nThis programme is centred on the chaconne: we hear how Henry Purcell and J. S. Bach join hands in this much-loved dance form of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. Of the few works of Philipp Heinrich Erlebach that survive, we perform a suite that concludes with a chaconne. The two Bach cantatas are contrasting: BWV 150 is said to be Bach''s earliest surviving cantata, BWV 78 was composed in Leipzig at the height of his career.\n\nRachel Elliott - soprano\nClare Wilkinson - alto\nNicholas Mulroy - tenor\nMatthew Brook - bass\nNicolette Moonen - violin\nRodolfo Richter - -violin\nAnne Schumann - violin / viola\nRachel Stott - viola\nAlison McGillivray - cello\nElizabeth Bradley - double bass\nMarion Moonen - flute\nJames Eastaway - oboe\nCatherine Latham - oboe\nAlastair Mitchell - bassoon\nSilas Standage - organ / harpsichord\n\n"There''s something about the openness of sound, the sheer quality of music-making and the sense of connection between performers and composers that makes this a very special recording. Its contents explore the world of the Baroque chaconne, a secular dance form that became a staple of music for the concert room and chapel. The challenge of creating a piece above a repeated bass line certainly appealed to Bach, Purcell and the little-known Erlebach, each on inspired, inventive form in the works offered here. The Bach Players ... reach the music''s emotional heart with tremendous conviction."\nAS, Classic FM magazine, January 2010\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bach Players/Every One a Chaconne/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bach Players/Every One a Chaconne/cover_200.jpg', 1313823600, 30),
(1418, 577, 'Italy versus France', 'Italian or French? Jean Baptiste Lully and Arcangelo Corelli were the champions of these two musical styles and the main subjects of the many attempts to establish which style was better. Or could the two styles be united? This programme paints a musical picture of the seventeenth century in Europe, through the music of Corelli, Lully, and their contemporaries.\n\nChief among the reconcilers was the organist and composer Georg Muffat. German but of Scottish ancestry, it was he who introduced both styles to Germany. Also included are Rebel''s homage to Lully and Couperin''s to Corelli.\n\n"This is the most enjoyable programme of seventeenth-century chamber music I have heard in a long time. The recording manages to capture the strings in gutty rather than silvery detail so we relax and enjoy the music without suffering from steel-string fatigue. Altogether, this is a wonderfully diplomatic programme which brings the best out in everyone - French, Italian, German and performers alike."\n- Simon Heighes\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bach Players/Italy versus France/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bach Players/Italy versus France/cover_200.jpg', 1310194800, 27),
(1419, 577, 'Nun Komm', 'Nun komm! explores the form of the French overture in two of Bach''s cantatas and in a piece by a predecessor: Philipp Heinrich Erlebach. The Bach Players gather their fullest forces to perform the two thrilling cantatas. Interspersed is Isaac''s ''Innsbruck, ich muss dich lassen'', sung a capella and in two instrumental settings.\n\nJ.S. Bach, Cantata ''Nun komm der heiden Heiland'', BWV 61\nPhilipp Heinrich Erlebach, Suite\nHeinrich Isaac, ''Innsbruck, ich muss dich lassen''\nJ.S.Bach, Cantata ''In allen meinen Taten'', BWV 97\n\n"... this quite magical disc. Intimate, infused with joy and overflowing with genuine affection, this is music-making which combines enthusiasm and craftsmanship to create something both rare and precious. ... these are performances which stand out from the crowd for their freshness, vitality and sense of total commitment to the music"\nMarc Rochester - International Record Review, November 2010\n\n"This is an admirable recording: intelligently conceived and well performed. Both cantatas open with a chorus in the shape of a French overture: in no. 61 it''s the slow introduction that incorporates phrases of the chorale, whereas in no. 97 the singers don''t enter until the Vivace. The Erlebach piece, published in 1693, consists of a French overture followed by the usual suite of dances. The other theme of the disc is Heinrich Isaac''s ''Innsbruck ich muss dich lassen'', heard as a vocal quartet and as instrumental fantasias by Christian Hollander and Paul Lutkeman; and, lo and behold, it''s the very tune that becomes the chorale melody at the beginning and end of ''In allen meinen Taten''. ... Nicolette Moonen plays brilliantly, as she does in the tenor aria of no. 97, with its roulades and double-stopping. Even if you don''t like Bach sung one-to-a-part, you can''t fail to enjoy these performances."\nRichard Lawrence - Gramophone, December 2010\n\n* IRR Outstanding disc (November 2010)\n* BBC Radio 3, CD Review Disc of the Week (December 2010)\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bach Players/Nun Komm/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bach Players/Nun Komm/cover_200.jpg', 1300431600, 24),
(1420, 578, 'The Bell Hours', 'The music on "The Bell Hours" is an eclectic and sentimental take on modern Rock and Roll, taking lyrical themes from more modern times and ideas with our impressions of love, compassion, and mindfulness (not to mention anger and jealousy) and combined it with musical themes stretching from Folk, Rock and Roll, Indie, Hip Hop, and Jazz, culminating in a rich pop music experience with amazing replay value.\n\nThe group of talented musicians inspired by the music that came together to tour the album have done an astounding job in translating the dynamics and moods live that the album captured in its recordings.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bell Hours/The Bell Hours/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bell Hours/The Bell Hours/cover_200.jpg', 1416729600, 96),
(1421, 578, 'Easy Weather', 'The music of The Bell Hours is an eclectic and sentimental take on modern Rock and Roll. Taking lyrical themes from more modern times and ideas with our impressions of love, compassion, and mindfulness (not to mention anger and jealousy) and combining them with musical themes stretching from Folk, Rock and Roll, Indie, Hip Hop, and Jazz, it culminates in a rich pop music experience with amazing replay value.\n\nThe group of talented musicians inspired by the music that came together to tour the album have done an astounding job in translating the dynamics and moods live that the album captured in its recordings.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bell Hours/Easy Weather/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bell Hours/Easy Weather/cover_200.jpg', 1426921200, 41),
(1422, 579, 'Now is The Time', 'Awesomely nerdy new wave here folks. There''s something incredibly great about the lyrics of The Bots'' fun, driving techno. Verses that would be super cheesy if delivered by a carbon based life form become delightfully campy and even a little fascinating when sung by "Synthia." When she utters phrases like "You''ve got to keep on pushing to a higher level" in the first track or "A good groove makes a captive of me" in her clipped, disaffected computer voice, it sounds precisely the way an android band would if they were earnestly trying to imitate club music. Whether it means to or not, this music ends up being a great parody of human beings'' attempts to be devastatingly sexy and cool, and it just works somehow. It''s also fantastic workout music.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bots/Now is The Time/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bots/Now is The Time/cover_200.jpg', 1085036400, 5),
(1423, 579, 'Truth_', 'The Bot''s brand of awesomely nerdy new wave/techno gets even better with Truth, a driving dance album featuring the world''s first virtual band. "Synthia" gets slightly darker with the lyrics on this one, and we really feel the cyber-pathos when she frets that "the world is changing so fast around me" or that she''s "trying to make this life worth living." We also particularly love that she says she''s "just trying to keep it real," or when she announces that "she''s losing control" in her utterly detached tones ("Don''t Funk With The Funk Junkie"). Yes, folks, it''s true: Synthia''s charms go way beyond the cyber-double-Ds that her creator dreamed up for her. Check out The Bots today.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bots/Truth_/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Bots/Truth_/cover_200.jpg', 1085036400, 6),
(1424, 580, 'Everything Comes to Those Who Wait', '''Everything comes to those who wait'' by Celtic Reggae Revolution features a mix of reggae versions of traditional Irish songs and original Celtic/Reggae fusions. Well loved traditional Irish songs ''Raggle Taggle Gypsy'' and ''Spancil Hill'' are reworked in reggae style on, respectively, ''Raggle Taggle Reggae'' and ''Spancil Hill/Gordon Wilson is a forgiver''. Original tune compositions include ''reggae reels'' - a blend of reggae and traditional reels - ''Gandhi''s Triumph'' (with its delightfully syncopated rhythms) and ''The Very Hairy Rasta Fairy'' and Brian Gilligan singing the laid back ''You Got to Laugh'' and ''The United Mistakes of America'' (complete with Hillbilly banjo and reggae beat).\n\nThe lineup features Northern Irishman Jarlath Henderson, one of the finest exponents of the uilleann pipes in the World, he is a talented and versatile musician who also plays, low whistle, tin Whistle, flute and cittern in addition to having a fine voice. He regularly guests with Scottish bands Capercaille, Salsa Celtica and Phil Cunningham. Martin MacDonald from Lewis also features on guitar and his performances on "Good People" and "Raggle Taggle Reggae" in particular, are highlights of the album. Tom Roche from Cork, (Accordion) also features on some tracks with his "Sliabh Luachra" style and Egyptian Aly Salam features on drums and percussion.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Celtic Reggae Revolution/Everything Comes to Those Who Wait/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Celtic Reggae Revolution/Everything Comes to Those Who Wait/cover_200.jpg', 1420704000, 68),
(1425, 581, 'Shop Sessions', 'HandPans (or "Hang"), are a fairly new instrument. They were Invented in Switzerland in 2000. They''re a relative of the Steel Pan and are, as the name implies, designed to be played by hand.\n\nJudith and Colin connected in late 2013 and they''ve been playing together ever since. Inspired and delighted by an absolute passion for the beauty and resonance of the "singing steel," Colin and Judith are The Dimples.\n\nWhen starlings flock together, wheeling and darting through the sky in tight, fluid formations, it''s called a murmuration. Using basic melodic structures as framework for improvisation, The Dimples create murmurations of harmony and rhythm.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dimples/Shop Sessions/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dimples/Shop Sessions/cover_200.jpg', 1431414000, 63),
(1426, 582, 'Filmscore', 'The Dino Haak Collective''s Filmscore was conceived as the musical accompaniment to a tongue-in-cheek, yet grandiose commentary on the obscure side of existence. The band had a unified vision, to create music that transcends the usual and predictable and above all to continue their quest of finding the "perfect groove".\n\nOn board this time are German artist Dino Haak on the Mobius Megatar and vocals, DJ James Ho from Taiwan, Seattle''s own Artis the Spoonman (Soundgarden, Zappa, Audislave etc.) on spoons and Alex Westcoat (Say Hi!, David Banzan) on the drums. With "A herd of wild Elephants coming right at you", "You guys are so Star Wars", "and a Heavy Nintendo vibe", the music transcends a variety of genres and attracts fans of artists such as System of A Down, Peter Gabriel, King Crimson, The Prodigy and George Clinton.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dino Haak Collective/Filmscore/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dino Haak Collective/Filmscore/cover_200.jpg', 1272697200, 6),
(1427, 582, 'Switzerland Your Light Is On', 'With "Switzerland Your Light is On", The Dino Haak Collective is again staying true to their unique style and mission of creating the unusual. Like every DHC album, it has its own idea and conceptual script.\n\nS.Y.L.I.O can be seen as the band''s tough and gritty "American" album. It is heavy, it is dark, and in true DHC tradition, ever so focused on the groove. "Switzerland Your Light is On" comes with a healthy, charming dose of controlled anger along with the usual tongue-in-cheek humor the band is known for. On board with Dino Haak and The Collective this time are Troy Glessner on guitars, Sean Siner on drums with guest musicians Marino Corriea (Hammond organ) and Phil Peterson (cello, trumpet).\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dino Haak Collective/Switzerland Your Light Is On/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dino Haak Collective/Switzerland Your Light Is On/cover_200.jpg', 1281769200, 6),
(1428, 583, 'Black and White', '"The Dirty Love" definitely have Punk sensibilities and attitude, but the music is far more intricate than your average Punk band. There is a clear sound and excellent instrumental passages."\n\nThe band writes of this album:\nWhen writing Black and White we wanted to touch on subjects that really provoked us and just let people have it. I think its a diverse record in the way of styles, there is something for everyone on here. Its definitely a more raw album than our previous and has plenty of live feeling energy, which is something else we really wanted to do this time around. All in all its 10 songs of pure bliss, great to drive to and I think people will relate to the messages being said here.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dirty Love/Black and White/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dirty Love/Black and White/cover_200.jpg', 1311750000, 5),
(1429, 583, 'Hurricane Tide', 'Hurricane tide is a stripped back version of "The Dirty Love''s" normal hard rocking self. Featuring well written songs from the heart, loads of great melody and some monster guitar solos that make your ears just want to melt. It''s raw, and for the most part live: a must have for the classic rock/punk/acoustic music lover.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dirty Love/Hurricane Tide/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dirty Love/Hurricane Tide/cover_200.jpg', 1348383600, 6),
(1430, 583, 'Roulette Romance', 'We wrote Roulette Romance in ''09 and we were still pretty young. It has got a lot of attitude and speed about it and some really good catchy tracks that people seem to like. It is rock''n roll and it is pop and it even features a slow track with an acoustic guitar.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dirty Love/Roulette Romance/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Dirty Love/Roulette Romance/cover_200.jpg', 1317279600, 6),
(1431, 584, 'All Directions', 'All Directions is the first album by The Donnis Trio released in 2007. The album''s mix of rock, folk, and reggae, clearly showed they had a disregard for marketing and pigeon holing themselves into one specific style. However, the songwriting and feel of the music is top notch.\n\nFuelled by the songwriting of Nate Donnis and the production of Phil Woodring the music has honesty, simplicity, and complexity. A few of the songs off the album regularly receive over 1 million plays every 3 months on Pandora Radio. If your a Dave Matthews Fan, a Nick Drake Fan, or even Tom Waits your going to love this.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Donnis Trio/All Directions/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Donnis Trio/All Directions/cover_200.jpg', 1429081200, 54),
(1432, 584, 'Cole Pond', 'Most of the tracks on this album are drawn from the lens of nature and childhood memories. Driving in the summer to vacation at Cole Pond with the family or feeling the warmth of a fire lit room in the winter. The music sought to capture the magic of these experiences as they were so fondly recollected.\n\nThis is a great album to listen to while driving through the country and small towns or just something to put on at home when you want to have some wine and relax.\n\n"Cole Pond" is collection of folk songs released by The Donnis Trio in 2012.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Donnis Trio/Cole Pond/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Donnis Trio/Cole Pond/cover_200.jpg', 1431414000, 36),
(1433, 585, 'EP One', 'The Easton Ellises aim to create music for auditoriums as much as for dance floors. The result is a combination of side chained electronic music with a mix of rock n'' roll. A synthesizer making love to a Les Paul. A TR808 transgendered into a Ludwig supraphonic.\n\nIt''s all about sex, love, fantasies and addictions. Painted women, impossible love, liquor and machine guns.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Easton Ellises/EP One/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Easton Ellises/EP One/cover_200.jpg', 1304406000, 7),
(1434, 586, 'A Diary of Occurrences', 'A Diary of Occurrences EP is a collection of 7 tracks each composed to reflect an atmosphere or memory. Working again with loop based and system based composition techniques, The glimmer room have come up with the perfect antidote to the relative starkness of the previous album I Remain. The light and sometimes playful melodies float over a dreamy bed of recorded loops and sound manipulation, giving A Diary of Occurrences a very blurred, melancholic and distant atmosphere.\n\n"The main inspiration for this recording started with a dream I''d had, wherein I lived in a big and old rambling house that was set very much in keeping with a early 1900''s country house. This developed into a fascination with the inhabitants of the house and the stories and the memories that by now are lost in time forever".\nAndy Condon.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/the glimmer room/A Diary of Occurrences/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/the glimmer room/A Diary of Occurrences/cover_200.jpg', 1301122800, 11),
(1435, 586, 'grey mirrors', 'Grey Mirrors is the second album from the unique and groundbreaking the glimmer room. It builds on what has gone before but this time the atmosphere is everything. A gentle bird song beckons you into the piece and what follows is a 42 minute, epic journey though layers of time and emotion.\n\nThe glimmer room''s strengths lie in the ability to present supremely melodic structures within a work with such emotional depth. In some passages you are cocooned in a warm luminescence, feeling safe and untouchable as lush choral washes wrap around you. Then the mood changes and you are thrown into an unsettling, barren and misty landscape where you are confronted by the sinister scraping of digital bits and bytes being smashed around a processor. As urgently as it arrives it is gone and you are left floating, clarified in a place of great beauty.\n\nHeralded as the new benchmark for the chill out album, Grey Mirrors is almost a meditation. But every journey is as individual as the listener.\n\nThe ambience of the piece has been likened to the long grandiose intros of Pink Floyd with the overpowering Englishness of John Foxx''s Cathedral Oceans. While musically comparisons range from Leftfield''s Melt, the KLF''s Chill Out, to the more chilled tracks from The Future Sound of London. Add to this a sprinkle of sound design from Delia Derbyshire and you will be close to the sound of this unique album.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/the glimmer room/grey mirrors/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/the glimmer room/grey mirrors/cover_200.jpg', 1218178800, 15),
(1436, 586, 'I remain', 'This album is a return to a more sparse sound, first heard on the band''s album "Grey Mirrors". It uses multiloop based systems similar to the systems of Brian Eno to build up the basic tracks before the more melodic elements where produced. This provided the machines the space they needed to breath and throw up some truly intriguing avenues that would not have been explored with a more conventional approach to the composition. Slow long ambient loops that always develop and mutate underpin the melancholic washes of sound and melodies.\n\nIn essence it is the album where the machines finally have became true friends. "I Remain" is an album presented as one continuous piece of music but split into 9 discrete movements.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/the glimmer room/I remain/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/the glimmer room/I remain/cover_200.jpg', 1273302000, 9),
(1437, 586, 'now we are six', 'Following on from 2004''s Grey Mirrors, this new release looks to all the lost jewels, the one off tracks composed with a purpose in mind, throughout The Glimmer Room''s first 6 years.\n\nThe Glimmer Room''s Andy picks up the story and the idea came about like most good ideas, sat round a table in a pub with a group of close friends. At one point in the evening he was asked, "what happened to all the tracks that never made it onto your albums?" he explained that a fair few had been used on magazine cover disks and compilations, but it started him thinking about all those pieces of music, six years worth of tracks spread all over the globe. What chance did people have of hearing all of them? With this thought still fresh in his mind, the next day he started to find all the old masters. Over the next few weeks he worked on mastering them and finding a running order that ran like an album and not just a collection of pieces.\n\nWhile he was working on this he started to get the feeling that he was bringing them all back home, safe under one roof. Now We Are Six contains a vast mixture of rare studio, live and re-mixed tracks, giving the listener a chance to hear some of The Glimmer Room''s most obscured from view, but totally inspired and atmospheric moments to date, in one sitting.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/the glimmer room/now we are six/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/the glimmer room/now we are six/cover_200.jpg', 1222239600, 12),
(1438, 587, 'Open Sezame', 'A darkly pleasing romp through industrial/electronic/hard-rock genres.\n\nThe artist (The Great Mooklaa Herashaa) writes: I am attempting to take the listener on a journey, a journey that mirrors my own, in that it follows my own inner search for the music inside to let itself out into reality.\n\nI start with more classic structured songs like Stomp and Discipline which stem from my own past in gothic and industrial music with a hint of BDSM bondage for flare.\n\nThen, add definite parts and arrangements in more experimental pieces with more emotion and unusual sounds and beats of their own such as Multch, Angel and Bloodsong.\n\nFinally, into the realm of trance like ambient states through pieces like Host Of Siafu and an almost psychedelic journey into the underworld itself with Syclicle and The Sterile InsomNiac.\n\nMy hope is that the listener when finished will have the feeling of discovering that something special that only music can bring.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Great Mooklaa Herashaa/Open Sezame/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Great Mooklaa Herashaa/Open Sezame/cover_200.jpg', 1275721200, 6),
(1439, 588, 'Apa Ya', 'Full of spice, color, and heat, Apa Ya fuses traditional world music with modern beats and highly advanced audio design to create music in entirely new, never-before-heard dialect of rhythm and sound. This album is a great example of how the world genre is moving into more sophisticated territory without losing any vibrancy. It''s one of those albums that simply must be heard to be appreciated.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Apa Ya/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Apa Ya/cover_200.jpg', 1148886000, 11),
(1440, 588, 'Bereru', 'Bereru is a diversified mixture of fresh and experimental songs between pop, rock and electronica.\n\nAgain, Andreas Ecker aka The Headroom Project fuses unusual sound and rhythmic performances with the advanced vocal design using contemporary special software.\n\nFurthermore, Bereru invites the listener to discover sounds never or rarely heard in a musical context, for example the noise of a cappuccino machine which represents the essential element of the beat on "Kiss Someday Babe". Or the crunch of old trees together with a shaman voice on "700 Miles to Nuuk", Watergongs and whispered voices in front of dark machine noises, extremely processed virtual blues guitar solo on "Vanesh da Ginda". Last but not least, the voices of Sigrid Haverkamp on "Funky Star" and "Forever", and Jaqueline Scheider on "I Will Follow" enrich the album with power, personality and charm.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Bereru/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Bereru/cover_200.jpg', 1323676800, 9),
(1441, 588, 'Ciri a Doro', 'Andreas Ecker delivers another batch of his incredibly charismatic, unique take on world music, here conceived as a sleek, futuristic mash-up of imaginary languages layered over mid-tempo electronic beats and jazz-tinged melodies. Artists making this kind of music typically cite bands like Dead Can Dance and Delirium as influences, but Ecker says his run more along the lines of King Crimson, Frank Zappa, and Peter Gabriel. You can really hear the humor and crazy energy of these influences in the music, which sounds like the lost soundtrack to The Fifth Element. A groovy, surprising album-full of exotic flavor and vivid atmospheres. Listen for the fantastic bass performance by Ecker too.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Ciri a Doro/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Ciri a Doro/cover_200.jpg', 1153378800, 7),
(1442, 588, 'COS', 'The seventh album of The Headroom Project is a homage of the charismatic and expressive voice of Anna B. who wrote all the lyrics, too. Anna B. was born in South Africa and traveled around the whole world. Today she lives in Munich, Germany. In the 90s she climbed the international charts with some Mainstream pop music hits. On this album, Anna exhibits her talent for a further range of musical expressiveness. Together with the powerful beats and unconventional instrumental arrangements, a very special sound of electronic rock is produced.\n\nAll the tracks were recorded in 2005 and have been completely re-mastered for release in 2010. The last song of this mini album is a bonus track in German.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/COS/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/COS/cover_200.jpg', 1297411200, 12),
(1443, 588, 'Dominus Illuminato Mea', '"Dominus Illuminato Mea" is the result of an outstanding collaboration between Andreas Ecker (aka "The Headroom Project)" and 5 charismatic artists: Liliana Flores, Jaqueline Schwarz, Peter M. Mahr, Eberhard Geitz and Otto Reiter.\n\nLiliana Flores, Mexico City: Vocals ? lyrics for track 10 "Kathrina". Liliana is an actrice, song writer and tv-moderator in Mexico. (She was on the screen for VH1 Latin America during 2004 and 2008.)\n\nJaqueline Schwarz, Vienna, Austria: Vocals on track 7 "selve amiche" and track 8 "halla hey". Jaqueline is a trained singer (classical and jazz) and earns her money by singing on funerals.\n\nPeter M. Mahr, Vienna, Austria: composition, arrangement, keys, sound programming, mix on track 5 "water meditation", track 6 "blue darkness", track 7 "selve amiche", track 8 "halla heij", composition, arrangement, keys, sound programming, mix and voice on track 9 "2k7". See petermmahr.com (blog).\n\nEberhard Geitz, Weserbergland, Germany: composition, arrangement, sound programming, keys on track 5 "water meditation", additional keys on track 6 "blue darkness", the incredible virtual guitar solo on track 8 "halla heij", drum programming ? virtual guitar on track 9 "2k7".\n\nOtto Reiter: fretless bass on track 8 "halla heij".\n\nAndreas Ecker, Bonn, Germany: drum programming on track 6 "blue darkness", drum programming and voice edition on track 7 "selve amiche", drum programming and keys on track 8 "halla heij". All instruments, arrangement, composition, mix on track 1-4 and 9-14.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Dominus Illuminato Mea/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Dominus Illuminato Mea/cover_200.jpg', 1234252800, 10),
(1444, 588, 'Haifa', 'Do you want to discover a never before heard sound? This album invites you to take a vivid and vibrant journey across the world of music - without borders or limits. Using the latest voodoo-studio-technology Andreas Ecker (aka The Headroom Project) merges hypnotic rhythms with expressive ethno vocals and hyper-real instruments to create extraordinary music beyond all known genres. Alpine Choirs, Polynesian war-shouts, Mongolian overtone-singers, fragile, morbid and heartbreaking guitar-riffs are only a few of the stations you will pass during this totally unique musical journey.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Haifa/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Haifa/cover_200.jpg', 1257580800, 11),
(1445, 588, 'Jetuton Andawai', 'Combining amazing sound design with music from Africa, the Middle East, and Asia, Jetuton Andawai is one of the most vibrant albums we''ve heard this year. With dazzling vocals, fantastic bass work, and studio voodoo that''s just completely off the hook, it doesn''t sound like anything else out there. "Linda Morena" is the graceful, near-a cappella intro, and things proceed to get more and more interesting after that. "Jetuton Andawai" sounds like a Bollywood record being played backwards to Peter Gabriel, and "Lost World" is even more trippy, with tumbling, echoey loops and driving bass. "Manu Yu" has a mellow yet infectious groove, and the excellent "Copeuse Concon" combines future-classical Indian vocals with smoky jazz. None of the influences here seem like they should work together (islandy xylophones in a blues track?), but they not only succeed, they succeed wildly for a potent, totally unique sound. "Yesterday" is the most radio friendly track on the album, with pretty vocals by Sigrid Havercamp. But in true Headroom fashion, most of the vocals of Jetuton Andawai are shaped and manipulated to the point where they form new, never before heard languages. It''s world pop for the global village.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Jetuton Andawai/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Jetuton Andawai/cover_200.jpg', 1179212400, 9),
(1446, 588, 'In Kananga', 'In Kananga transports the heat, pure power and color of West/Central Africa between Tanger (Marocco) and Kananga (Kongo). There we meet the famous medicine man.\n\nThe inseparable force of rhythm and harmonies creates floating pictures you never imagined before. The track "How to ride an elephant" explains better that any special elephant-user-guide how to do what it says.\n\nThe song "Cutzi" is sung in Purepecha (the language of the Central Mexican Indians) and is an entrance to deeper meditation. Perhaps you will find here even more spiritual power than in the wise words of the medicine man. "Lovedrive" is a rare but at the same time impressive dance somewhere between industrial and trip hop.\n\nAndreas Ecker (aka The Headroom Project), designed new instrument phrases by separating the overtones from existing samples and treating them like independent notes, changing their time-lapses and pitch-curves. You can hear the results on "fragile piano suffering under micro organismas" or on "Gerome" (where the percussion hits in the background).\n\nAndreas Ecker: all instruments, arrangements, mixes. Vocals on "In Kananga"\nLiliana Flores: Vocals on "Cutzi" and "Fuimos Cosmos"\nSigrid Haverkamp: Vocals on "Lovedrive" and "Outside"\nCoverphoto: Jorn Daberkow (Landscape: Yoshua Tree National Park)\nCover Design: Andreas Ecker\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/In Kananga/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/In Kananga/cover_200.jpg', 1272092400, 14),
(1447, 588, 'Polaris', 'Polaris combines ethno vocals with sometimes smooth, sometimes massive electronic beats and a huge range of wide-ranging instruments. World percussion meets rare and strange tone generators like ancient glass bowls or conch shell horns.\n\nMost of the vocals consist of cutted and recomposed syllables of exotic languages - except "Let there be silence", sung by Sigrid Haverkamp. The tracks 7 to 11 are experimental rhythmic soundscapes including song-like fragments, shaman singer (Polaris) or slow moving trombones over a hypntoic monotone bass (Roll ? Roll ? Roll).\n\nAndreas Ecker: all instruments, arrangements, mixes\nSigrid Haverkamp: vocals on "Let there be Silence"\nArik Lucian Kami Ecker: baby breath-noises on "Polaris"\nCover Design: Andreas Ecker\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Polaris/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Headroom Project/Polaris/cover_200.jpg', 1371193200, 13),
(1448, 589, 'La Follia Itinerante', 'La Follia Itinerante. The itinerant madness. Der Fahrende Wahnsinn.\n\nIt is that moment in time and place when the soul catches a slow motion glimpse of itself traveling willy-nilly through the maelstrom of the great vortex. At once noble and base, it is coloured in a chromatic blue melancholy and dry red pathos. There too is the peace that comes with acceptance of one''s fate... and the anguished impossibility of not struggling against this same fate... La Follia Itinerante holds up a mirror that we may see ourselves as we really are: beggars before God. And makes us laugh at our own pitiful and ludicrous condition.\n\nA true Follia is unique at each playing. It exists for a moment and then vanishes. This one was caught on a warm and sunny afternoon in a garden house on the southern slopes of the Schwarzwald. It takes as inspiration the Follias of Nicholas Chedeville, Corelli, Vivaldi, Marais and the many other composers, known and unknown, who have added to our treasure store of Follias.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Hurdy-Gurdy Band/La Follia Itinerante/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Hurdy-Gurdy Band/La Follia Itinerante/cover_200.jpg', 1275030000, 11),
(1449, 590, 'Italian Cantatas and Sonatas (GF Handel and Caldara)', 'Two of the most dramatic and emotional cantatas of the Baroque period for alto voice share this album with a colorful variety of sonatas from Handel and Caldara. Countertenor Jay Carte joins the Kingsbury Ensemble in this celebration of two of the most successful of Italian opera: GF Handel and Antonio Caldara.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Kingsbury Ensemble/Italian Cantatas and Sonatas (GF Handel and Caldara)/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Kingsbury Ensemble/Italian Cantatas and Sonatas (GF Handel and Caldara)/cover_200.jpg', 1258790400, 14),
(1450, 591, 'Berlin', 'Ok, Berlin is just so damn sexy. Angular, androgynous, pouty?it''s a Bowie/Siouxsie-influenced urchin of an album that''ll have you strutting around the house like a Milanese model on a Fashion Week catwalk. Highlights include the smouldering opener "Impressions," the world''s only New Wave drinking song "We Didn''t Go," and the deliciously disaffected "Tap At Floes." Skeptics take note: this is not the trend-hopping New Wave of the last few years; this the direct descendent of New Wave...its legitimate heir.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Kokoon/Berlin/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Kokoon/Berlin/cover_200.jpg', 1166601600, 9),
(1451, 591, 'Erase', 'For a decidedly new wave album, there''s a lot of passion and emotion on Erase. Danyx''s brooding, Nico-esque voice has a searching quality about it, and there are some great moments when it breaks out of its stylized discontent to become genuinely melodic and, fleetingly, joyous. Recommended tracks include "Face," "Delicate," the Cure-tinged "Tied," and "Take Me to the World," a bittersweet but ultimately upbeat song that hints at what''s to come on The Kokoon''s incredible follow-up Berlin.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Kokoon/Erase/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Kokoon/Erase/cover_200.jpg', 1083135600, 8),
(1452, 591, 'No 3', 'The tunes on No.3 are catchy, full of hooks and energy while Danyx'' voice shows sensitivity and emotions in a way never heard before. The headling song "I''d love to go on" might be the first - yes! - love song they''ve ever done but without the missing of tragedy and beautiful suffering in a shape of big emotion. "No.3" covers a new step in the music biography of The Kokoon.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Kokoon/No 3/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Kokoon/No 3/cover_200.jpg', 1308380400, 7),
(1453, 592, 'The Lost Mode', 'Medieval music meets living modal music traditions: The Lost Mode features an eclectic set of music from the Middle Ages, as well as melodies from Sephardic, North African, Armenian, Breton, and Basque modal traditions. Our interpretations are meant to extend, complement, and bridge the gap between written sources and oral transmission.\n\nNew compositions based on medieval models stand next to their century-old counterparts. Some melodies are lifted out of their original context, presented in a different meter, or brought to life on instruments that would not have been known in the music''s original culture. The result: a multi-layered, time-and boundary-crossing collection of tones, timbres, textures, lovingly held together by the common thread of mode.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Lost Mode/The Lost Mode/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Lost Mode/The Lost Mode/cover_200.jpg', 1295078400, 15),
(1454, 593, 'Crisis', 'CRISIS is the brain child of Los Angeles based artist Ajax Garcia and NEWSTED drummer Jesus Mendez Jr.\n\nDuring The Napoleon Blownaparts US tour stint supporting KISS and WEEZER back in 2001 the band had plans to record their long awaited followup to their 1998 album "Thrill Hype" but due to conflicting schedules plans to record were put on hold as drummer Jesus Mendez Jr. had prior commitments to his other band NEWSTED (see Jason Newsted of METALLICA).\n\nMendez and Ajax along with new recruit Eric Griffin (MURDER DOLLS) on bass guitar finally entered the studio to begin tracking but because of musical differences the band never got passed the recordings of just two cover tracks with Griffin as part of the line up. The two tracks that were recorded were ultimately released as part of Jetboys Of Babylon (a tribute to The New York Dolls album) which featured David Johansen himself and Steve Jones of The Sex Pistols. Ajax, Mendez and Griffin contributed a blistering version of the Dolls classic Puss In Boots.\n\nGriffin''s replacement was bassist Doug Wellmon a young punk rocker from Hollywood California and a former member of the band Pipedown.\n\nWhen the two musicians finally began tracking they recruited a number of musicians to guest on what would become the CRISIS album including legendary blues musicians John Clifton on Harmonica and the legendary Edward Burke on piano. The 70 year old Edward Burke also played with the Ike and Tina Turner Review in the 1970''s. Long time friend and guitarist Jessie Farnsworth also of NEWSTED completed the line up and CRISIS was finally released in 2010. AMERICA''S TIRE company used the opening track HELL YEAH as part of their national advertising campaign and the UK CLASSIC ROCK MAGAZINE picked up on the second single MY SPACE PICTURES for their tribute to Guns N'' Roses insert Sons Of Guns.\n\nSonically CRISIS is a raw mix of punk rock and garage rock and roll with heavy doses of soul and the blues. Drawing influences from the likes of The Rolling Stones, New York Dolls, The Stooges and heavy tip of the hat to Chuck Berry the CRISIS album takes you on a dark trip down a seedier side of Hollywood California not advertised to tourists.\n\nThe classic slash and burn guitars, frantic drumming under the impressive vocal range of guitarist/vocalist Ajax Garcia make up this low-fi gem.\n\nRecognized for his powerful, raw, biting but truthful commentary on our cultural and social condition, Ajax'' work on the song "Hedonistic Babies" resonates on every level! Garcia is a post postmodern rarity of authentic swagger and angst, continually serving up his Fresno-born and Hollywood-bred artistry on his own terms. Crisis Starts Now!\nMusic and street art photographer Karin Lindberg Freda, 2013\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Napoleon Blown Aparts/Crisis/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Napoleon Blown Aparts/Crisis/cover_200.jpg', 1405062000, 32),
(1455, 593, 'Thrill Hype', 'Biting guitars and gritty, pissed off rock vocals anyone? Yes please. Napoleon Blown Aparts are like a punked up Guns ''N Roses with a dash of The Dolls (See "Unlucky Star"). Ajax Garcia''s voice sounds absolutely great on this album, and we can''t get enough of his crystal clear, spot-on guitar work either. These guys are clearly rock purists. We like-y. Oh and check out "Sonny" for a totally unexpected combination of bongo drums and "motherfucker" references.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Napoleon Blown Aparts/Thrill Hype/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Napoleon Blown Aparts/Thrill Hype/cover_200.jpg', 1079596800, 13),
(1456, 594, 'Keep Your Head Up', 'This album is packed with gritty grooves, sensual female vocals, funky bass and drums and old style keys. A combination of vocal tracks and purely instrumental tunes inspired by 70''s cop show soundtracks and Funk Club music take the listener back to a time of car chases through litter strewn alleys, Afro haircuts, purple flairs and smoky bars. So, kick back and enjoy...\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Old Recruits/Keep Your Head Up/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Old Recruits/Keep Your Head Up/cover_200.jpg', 1423382400, 58),
(1457, 595, 'Public Service Announcement', 'John writes: Keith Adams is a professional drummer who--on the side--writes really perfect pop rock that make me smile.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Proper Authorities/Public Service Announcement/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Proper Authorities/Public Service Announcement/cover_200.jpg', 1231488000, 3),
(1458, 595, 'Public Service Announcement (Instrumental)', 'This instrumental version of the Proper Authorities'' 2009 Magnatune release "Public Service Announcement" provides a modern pop rock bed of groove, drama, intensity, and fun. With the exception of the opening track, the originally lyric-free track "The Supermachine Looks Much Bigger on TV," this collection of songs focuses on a non-vocal soundscape of various moods and timbres. Sometimes playful, sometimes serene, sometimes epic, sometimes melancholic, these tracks are always interesting.\n\nWhen discussing the approach to writing the original album, Proper Authorities'' brainchild Keith Adams described its primary theme as "how time affects our approaches to living... a song about ''You can accomplish anything you want'' has more weight and realism to it when you add ''But you''d better step up to the task before it''s too late.''"\n\nThough the words are absent, the urgency and attitude of these songs is still very much present. The hard-edged guitar work and no-holds-barred drums of the track "Losing The Now" evokes a feel of a military march with victorious and timely purpose. The electro-rock synth stylings and slinky bass work of "Make A Choice For Me" suggest a cold, urban environment and an immediacy of action required. And songs like "The Ninth Life Jitters" and "Whodunit" are just plain fun rock songs! Enjoy "Public Service Announcement: Instrumental"!\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Proper Authorities/Public Service Announcement (Instrumental)/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Proper Authorities/Public Service Announcement (Instrumental)/cover_200.jpg', 1294473600, 4);
INSERT INTO `albums` (`id`, `artist_id`, `name`, `description`, `cover`, `cover_small`, `release_date`, `popularity`) VALUES
(1459, 596, 'One Armed Bandits', 'One Armed Bandits is a multi-faceted menu of beat-heavy, darkened R''n''B/Soul, with each song having a distinctly different influence and flavor from the next. There are wide variations, from the funky "Stuck in RED", to the ethereal "My Medicine", to the highly danceable "Slight of Hand" and "But You''re Mine", the dirge-like "Daddy May I", and the rockin'' "Ain''t the Madonna, Ain''t Your Whore".\n\nHowever, even with this wide a palette, the group never loses its own cohesive, identifiable sound. It remains consistent across the board, with wah-wah guitars, saucy horns, liberal use of electronics and samples, and sultry vocals. With all its cohesive range, "One Armed Bandits" delivers.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Raindoggs/One Armed Bandits/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Raindoggs/One Armed Bandits/cover_200.jpg', 1349938800, 4),
(1460, 597, 'Back Home', 'True fans of country music will find the perfect marriage of traditional and modern country in The Ranchhands'' "Back Home" album.\n\nWell crafted songs, a powerful and heartfelt vocal delivery by Mickey Kennedy, electrifying fiddle playing by Chris Tedesco, and lush 3 and 4 part vocal harmonies make this record soar. Glowing reviews from Texas to France gave this album and band international acclaim and a worldwide fanbase that continues to grow.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Ranchhands/Back Home/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Ranchhands/Back Home/cover_200.jpg', 1346050800, 9),
(1461, 597, 'Driven', 'The most recent release from The Ranchhands has solidified their position as a worldwide force on the country music scene.\n\nDriven embodies the best elements of today''s country music, featuring the rocking fiddle playing of Chris Tedesco, and the star-quality vocal performance of Mickey Kennedy.\n\nThe songs cover a wide range of emotions and settings, from getting lost in a vacation daydream, to the sudden loss of a loved one, to the comical everyday stories at a small town diner, to a song about a rockin country Friday night in the heart of Texas. While keeping true to country music, this albm will appeal to music fans of many different genres.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Ranchhands/Driven/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Ranchhands/Driven/cover_200.jpg', 1358236800, 8),
(1462, 598, 'Garden of Fugitives', 'Garden of Fugitives continues the theme of creating electronic music with a heart and soul. At times more introspective and reflective than their previous "10 Raptures" album, but still with tracks of power, rhythm and melody. From harsh, distorted leads to sweet, soaring strings, from soft acoustic drums to club spec beats Garden of Fugitives is a journey through all that electronic music can offer. If you thought you didn''t like electronic, take a listen and think again.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/Garden of Fugitives/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/Garden of Fugitives/cover_200.jpg', 1341385200, 9),
(1463, 598, 'Welcome to Mania', 'Welcome to Mania continues to explore electronic music with a heart and mixes melodic, organic phrases with electro dance and dubstep inspired bass and rhythm. Themes of contrast and change are explored by combining and juxtaposing old and new, rhythmic and melodic, syncopation and straight. Encompassing darker, dirtier moods than the two previous albums in the trilogy, Welcome to Mania completes the journey through the head of ThE rApTuRe...for now.....\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/Welcome to Mania/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/Welcome to Mania/cover_200.jpg', 1354608000, 10),
(1464, 598, 'Rush', 'On this album I wanted to explore the current fashion for analogue synthesis and pure analogue sounds, placed against different styles of modern dance music but also continuing my efforts to write electronic music which takes the listener on a journey in every song. This album was six months in the making; written, recorded and produced in the second half of 2013.\n\nThe 11 tracks include rave, trip-hop, trance, rap and drum ? bass influenced music unified by electrifying, screaming leads, pounding drums and complex arrangements, interspersed with more vocal sections than in previous albums.\n\nPlay this on your iPod in the gym and run faster, jump higher and hit harder. Play it on your car radio and I can''t be held responsible for your speeding fines! Please enjoy...Rush.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/Rush/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/Rush/cover_200.jpg', 1400310000, 19),
(1465, 598, '10 Raptures', 'Taking inspiration from early Jean Michel Jarre and 80''s analogue synth sounds, then combining contemporary dance beats and rhythms with a classical approach to form and structure, the album seeks to take dance music out of the clubs and place it firmly into a listening environment.\n\nThe tracks on 10 Raptures are in equal part dance, ambient and trance with a dose of electro thrown in for good measure. 10 Raptures was written, recorded and produced over the course of 10 months from Spring 2011 onwards. Using a multitude of hardware and software synthesizers, samples, drum machines and effects, the tracks were written, recorded, produced and mastered in-house at Mount Street Studio near London.\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/10 Raptures/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/10 Raptures/cover_200.jpg', 1331622000, 8),
(1466, 598, '2000Hz', 'Taking the listener on a journey through trance, dub, drum and bass and back, it''s best played loud; preferably in a dark club with lots of flashing lights and smoke.\n\nAlternatively, a phone and headphones will do just fine...whichever way you listen, please enjoy more electronica with a heart. 2000Hz is the fourth album from UK producer "The Rapture".\n', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/2000Hz/cover_400.jpg', 'http://he3.magnatune.com/music/The Rapture/2000Hz/cover_200.jpg', 1373180400, 14),
(1467, 599, 'House of Weather', 'The reminding ideas are brought about by the use of pressure, command, and authorization. They bring to mind a human being''s product of mental activity and high levels of industrialized standards of living by combining all of one''s characteristics. There are good ideas and bad ideas. There are rules, and there are animals. Some vegetables can bring you happiness. Other foods can give you just one final remaining idea. Whatever the case may be, you will save the day. This is where we all take a deep breath and wait for August to appear again and again and again and again and again.\n\nVegetables\nSpending thoughts make us forget everything. The notes we save will never be read. You may disagree, but plates don''t. Circle around and around the world until we all catch the fever. Let''s give the dog a bath. Let''s give ourselves a bath. Don''t forget to open the envelope before we jump in. This envelope contains the word of the day. Positively, nothing will go as planned. Nowhere can we run. Nowhere can we hide. We water the earth. We water the mouths. Unacceptable.\n\nRules for Animals\nWe weren''t suppose to be reasoning with life. We demand rules. No one should go through this. Bitched again and again. Flies are for flying, not dropping. Doors are for opening. Remember readers. Remember the word of the day. Mark it down forever. We are grabbing our belongings and coming for you now. Stay put and hide. Then, it is your job to get us out. Show us a way out, please.\n\nDo You Understand?\nBoom Down. Boom Down. Can you hear me? Again down. Boom Down. I''m calling your name. Unbelievable down. All down. Again down. I can hear you. Down boom down. Bow down again down. Got you. Go\n\nAugust\nAttention, Everyone here had disordered. This doesn''t feel real, but it is. Or is it? We got here from a flash of a light. It looks like we used to live here, but it''s different. Everyone sides with everyone. Sometimes there is a glitch and a restar